    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-035", "title": "36", "content": "36\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2023The global fashion industry is estimated to \nemploy 430 million people, or 12.6% of the global \nworkforce59. This profound contribution to global \neconomic activity is driven by the labour intensity \nof fashion production and the many layers of value \nadding activity that contribute to the development \nand supply of fashion products. \nDespite the dominance of fashion retailing within \nthe Kingdom and its continued dependence on \nimports, there are many small-scale manufacturers \noperating in Saudi Arabia. For example, domestic \nclothing and textiles manufacturi", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-036", "title": "37", "content": "37\nThe State of Fashion in the Kingdom of Saudi ArabiaData from the Saudi Central Bank indicates that \nthe apparel manufacturing portion of the fashion \nsupply chain alone employed 52,611 people in \n2021. Following the Manufacturing Value Added \n(MVA) trend for clothing and textiles production, \nas depicted in Figure 14, this represents a decline \non the 80,367 people employed in 2016 (see \nFigure 14), but still a substantial base for further growth and development61. As further highlighted \nin Figure 14, 7,281 Saudis are employed in in the \nclothing manufacturing segment of the fashion \nsuppl", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-037", "title": "38", "content": "38\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2023Several Saudi brands have a deep history of \nproduction in the Kingdom\nAlmohaisen is a case in point. Established 60 \nyears ago in the capital city of Riyadh, the firm \nis still successfully designing, developing and \nmanufacturing jewellery. This family-owned \nbusiness employs a team of jewellery operators, \nproducing a range of fine jewellery for the Saudi \nand regional market. While acknowledging the \nchallenges of manufacturing jewellery in the \nKingdom, co-owner Ahmad Almohaisen indicates \nthe value created by the business is substantial, and \nthat the fir", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-038", "title": "39", "content": "39\nThe State of Fashion in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabiafile and updated when new fittings are required, \nallowing the firm to operate a tailoring-type service \nwithin a volume production environment. This has \nenabled major growth at Lomar over the last few \nyears. The firm presently manufactures 140,000 \nthobes and 14,000 abayas a year; and anticipates \nmajor market growth over the next few years. \nLomar has used its manufacturing capabilities as \na competitive strength in the domestic market. \nAs a result of its deep understanding of fabrics \nused and the thobe manufacturing process itself, \n", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-039", "title": "40", "content": "40\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2023Loai Naseem, a pioneer in the traditional Saudi men’s \napparel market, has received multiple accolades, including \ntwo wins on the prestigious Saudi Fast Growth Startup \n100 list. In 2014, Forbes ranked Loai #2 on its list of \nLeaders Inspiring Saudi’s Entrepreneurial Elite. In an \nexclusive interview, Loai shares his insights on how Lomar \nwas established and how the company is leveraging \ntechnology to shape its future. Loai Naseem\nFounder of T afaseel International \nT rading Limited company (Lomar)\nT ell us more about yourself and \nhow Lomar was founded.\nI g", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-040", "title": "41", "content": "41\nThe State of Fashion in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabiaproblem is that custom-made \nthobes can take up to two weeks \nto be manufactured and some \npeople simply don’t want to wait \nthat long. It’s also to do with the \nprice-point: ready-made thobes \nare much cheaper and so a lot \nmore people want to buy these. \nNow, fast delivery and cheaper \nprices are strong selling points. \nSo, I imagine the market will shift \nmore and more toward ready-\nmade in the next decade.\nLomar uses innovations such as \nnano technology, to create new \nvalue in its product lines. T ell \nus a bit more about what you \ncur", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-041", "title": "42", "content": "42\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2023Near-shoring refers to the relocation \nof global supply chains closer to the \npoint of final market consumption. \nThe advantages of this approach \ninclude increased full price sales, less \nmarkdowns, less slow-moving stock \nin expensive selling spaces, improved \nstock turns and operating margins, \nand ultimately stronger business \nperformance.\nHaving the right mix of products in the right selling \nspace and at the right time appears to be a key \nchallenge for almost all Saudi retailers. Supply chain \nspeed and flexibility are widely recognised as key \ncompetiti", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-2023_Saudi_State_of_-042", "title": "43", "content": "43\nThe State of Fashion in the Kingdom of Saudi ArabiaPreviously, we highlighted the opportunity that \nexists for import substitution. Indeed, the local \nproduction displacement of only a fraction of \nthe US$7.3 billion (SAR 27.4 billion)  imported \nannually within the fashion value chain, opens \nhuge opportunities for firms in the domestic \nsupply chain, including manufacturers, logistics \nand transport service providers, and specialist \nmachinery suppliers. For example, even a 20% \ndisplacement of fashion imports into the Kingdom \nat 2021 levels would generate additional local \nmanufacturing", "source": "market/2023_Saudi_State_of_Fashion_Report.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
