    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-002", "title": "Saudi Arabiaʼs Vision 2030 and a Nation in ", "content": "Saudi Arabiaʼs Vision 2030 and a Nation in \nTransition \nAnnelle Sheline, Ph.D., Nonresident Fellow \nKristian Coates Ulrichsen, Ph.D., Fellow for the Middle East and Co-Director of the \nMiddle East Energy Roundtable \nThis publication was produced on behalf of Rice University’s Baker Institute for Public \nPolicy. Wherever feasible, the material was reviewed by external experts prior to its \nrelease. Any errors are the responsibility of the author(s) alone. \nThis material may be quoted or reproduced without prior permission, provided \nappropriate credit is given to the author(s) and Rice Universi", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-003", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 2 Saudi Arabiaʼs Vision 2", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 2 Saudi Arabiaʼs Vision 2030 and \na Nation in Transition \nIntroduction \nTen years have passed since Salman bin Abdulaziz Al Saud became King of Saudi \nArabia on Jan. 23, 2015, and transferred policymaking authority to a younger generation \nof princes led by his then 29-year-old son, Mohammed bin Salman. Fifteen months later, \nin April 2016, Prince Mohammed — by then Deputy Crown Prince as well as Minister of \nDefense — unveiled a comprehensive document called “Vision 2030” as a strategic \nframework for transforming the Saudi economy and so", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-004", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 3 transformation initiati", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 3 transformation initiatives and builds on the authors’ 2019 Baker Institute report, \n“Mohammed bin Salman and Religious Authority and Reform in Saudi Arabia.” Written \ntwo years after Mohammed bin Salman became Crown Prince, and at a time when he \nwas shunned by Western policymakers after the killing of Jamal Khashoggi in the Saudi \nConsulate in Istanbul in 2018, our earlier report assessed the balance between \nsymbolism and substance in Mohammed bin Salman’s early reforms, especially in the \nsocial sphere.6 \nThis new paper — published ne", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-005", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 4 was removed and the Sau", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 4 was removed and the Saudi state began to officially support women working outside \nthe home, in part by providing financial incentives to companies that hired women.12 \nBy 2023, Saudi Arabia had a female labor force participation rate of 34%, while the U.S. \nrate was 57% as of early 2025.13 The economic burden of strictly adhering to the \nkingdom’s previous religious orthodoxy had become too great, highlighting the interplay \nbetween the need for both societal and economic reform. Still, the decision to begin \nVision 2030 with a referenc", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-006", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 5 Strengthening Saudi Nat", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 5 Strengthening Saudi Nationalism \nWhile Vision 2030 begins by highlighting Islam, it quickly moves on to a core aspect of \nCrown Prince Mohammed’s agenda: both strengthening and altering Saudi nationalism. \nThe Saudi government has been engaged in various projects to center the ruling Al Saud \nfamily and decenter the role of Islam, some of which predated the Crown Prince’s rise to \npower. For example, King Abdullah established Saudi National Day in 2005, which at the \ntime was the only nonreligious annual holiday permitted by the state.19", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-007", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 6 A Changing Relationship", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 6 A Changing Relationship With Tourism \nAnother facet of how the Saudi state is revising its relationship with Islam is through its \nview of the territory’s pre-Islamic past, specifically the promotion of touristic sites. One \nof the goals in the first section of Vision 2030 is to “More than double the country’s \npresence on the UNESCO World Heritage List,” which it appears on track to \naccomplish.22 In 2016, Saudi Arabia had four UNESCO World Heritage sites; as of 2024 \nit had eight, with several more sites submitted for consideration. \nI", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-008", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 7 as Riyadh Season, many ", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 7 as Riyadh Season, many of which highlight different regions of the country while also \npromoting entertainment, including concerts, festivals, and other events. \nPromoting Culture and Entertainment \nThese recent tourism campaigns dovetail with another goal of Vision 2030: “Promoting \nCulture and Entertainment.” One underlying motivator of this goal was to encourage \ndomestic tourism. For Saudi women, in particular, travel abroad has long offered an \nopportunity for greater personal freedom than was possible at home. Now, the \ndevelopment", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-009", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 8 same as France attracte", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 8 same as France attracted in 2019, immediately before the pandemic.36 However, it \nemerged that the authorities were using an unconventional definition of the metric \n“visits .” Instead of counting individual travelers, they specified that “a person who visits \n10 destinations in the country during a trip will count for 10 visits.”37 Still, largely \npositive coverage from prominent outlets like The New York Times, Travel + Leisure, \nand Condé Nast — even if some of that coverage was sponsored by the Saudi Travel \nAuthority — suggests that", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-010", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 9 Several Vision 2030 pri", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 9 Several Vision 2030 priorit ies seek to build upon the locational and comparative \nadvantages that Saudi Arabia enjoys over other nearby countries, positioning the \nkingdom as a key player in the emerging chain of geo-economic hubs as the world \nmoves toward the midway mark of the 21st century. This is reflected in the focus \nplaced on the mining sector, as the kingdom seeks to draw on its largely untapped \ndomestic resources of minerals, integrate into evolving global transition supply chains, \nand invest heavily in processing facilitie", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-011", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 10 One of its most succes", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 10 One of its most successful endeavors to date has been the Savvy Games Group, which \nwas created as part of the PIF’s strategic plan for 2021–25 and built upon a string of \nprior PIF investments in esports and gaming companies. Savvy is consistent with the \nPIF’s desire to incubate national champions that can transform the kingdom into a \ncentral hub of an emerging sector — in this case, the global gaming industry and the \nbooming esports market. In June 2023, the CEO of Savvy told the Financial Times that , \n“The region is populated wit", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-012", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 11 However, these pragmat", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 11 However, these pragmatic (and much-needed) elements of Vision 2030 coexist \nalongside the giga-projects announced since 2017, projects that have dominated the \nkingdom’s policy focus, media attention, and funding streams, even as their economic \nutility remains largely untested. In the eyes of many — particularly those outside the \nkingdom — g iga-projects such as Neom have become synonymous with Vision 2030.60 \nOther signature giga-projects include Qiddiya, a giant entertainment complex outside \nRiyadh initially slated to open in 2023 ", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-013", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 12 economic advisors have", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 12 economic advisors have privately warned senior policy makers that the kingdom needs \nelevated oil prices for the next five years to keep spending billions of dollars on projects \nthat have so far attracted meager investment from abroad.”68 As of March 2025, the \nInternational Monetary Fund projected that the kingdom needed a breakeven oil price of \n$91 per barrel to balance the budget, as the actual price hovered around $70 per \nbarrel.69 \nSaudi officials responded to shortfalls in oil revenue and foreign investment by tapping \nlocal an", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-014", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 13 and opened in phases r", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 13 and opened in phases rather than all at once. Whereas The Line was originally planned \nto extend over 170 kilometers (approximately 106 miles), reports in 2024 suggested \nthat only 2.4 kilometers (1.5 miles) would be completed by 2030 — a timeline that may \nnow be pushed to 2034.77 Interim plans to construct 16 kilometers (10 miles) of The \nLine would, according to one report, have “meant constructing the equivalent of all the \noffice buildings in Midtown Manhattan three times over in a decade” and “ require \nsignificant portions of the", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-015", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 14 The travails of KAEC a", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 14 The travails of KAEC and other pre-2016 initiatives such as the King Abdullah Financial \nDistrict (KAFD) were noted in the original 2016 Vision 2030 document, which \nacknowledged that work had started at KAFD “without consideration of its economic \nfeasibility. ”85 However, a key difference between the pre-2016 projects and the Vision \n2030-related initiatives is that the latter have the backing of the leadership at the \nhighest level in the form of the Crown Prince and PIF, which was not always \nforthcoming in previous eras of developm", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-20250724_Sheline_and-016", "title": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 15 Notes ", "content": "Rice University’ s Baker Institute for Public Policy | 15 Notes \n1 Vision 2030 is a comprehensive document and strategic framework for achieving \nSaudi Arabia\'s long-term goals as a kingdom. It details plans to diversify its economy, \nprimarily to reduce its dependence on oil; develop a more knowledge-based society that \nincludes an enhanced quality of life and improved public services; and create a more \ntransparent and accountable government (Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, “Vision 2030 \nOverview,” accessed June 25, 2025, https://www.vision2030.gov.sa/en/overview). \n2 “Giga-project” is a term for l", "source": "market/20250724-Sheline and Ulrichsen-Vision 2030-Research Paper_0.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
