    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-001", "title": "1THE ", "content": "1THE \nST A TE OF \nFASHION \nSECTOR \nIN SAUDI \nARABIA \n2025", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-002", "title": "2FOREWORD", "content": "2FOREWORD\nCEO Message\n1. Saudi Arabia: A Strong and Growing Fashion Hub\nDEMAND\n2. A Resilient Consumer Base: Decoding and Engaging the Saudi Consumer\n3. Capturing the Saudi Consumer: Localizing Global Brands, Elevating Saudi Identity\n4. Cultural Heritage: The Future of Saudi Style\n5. Omni-Channel and Digital Engagement\nSUPPL Y\n6. Robust Retail Landscape and Infrastructure: The Key Role of Malls\n7. Building Local Capacity: Manufacturing and Skills\n8. Sustainability and Circular Economy: Local Actions, Global Impact\nDEEP DIVE\n9. Riyadh Fashion Week: A New Driver of Saudi T ourism\n10. Wellness, L", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-003", "title": "3", "content": "3\nCEO MESSAGE \nMarking five years since the founding of the Saudi Fashion Commission \ninvites both reflection and anticipation. What began as a mandate to build the \nfoundations of a national fashion sector has evolved into a broader mission \nto shape a creative economy rooted in identity and resilience. This year, the \nfashion industry in Saudi Arabia is expected to reach USD 34 billion, reaffirming \nthe Kingdom’s position as the fastest growing market in the GCC.\nY et the numbers only tell part of the story. What they reflect is the energy of a \nnew generation of designers and entrepreneurs,", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-004", "title": "4", "content": "4\nSAUDI ARABIA: \nA STRONG \nAND GROWING \nFASHION HUB\nSaudi Arabia’s fashion \nmarket continues to surge \nahead, experiencing rapid \ngrowth that is solidifying the \nKingdom’s leadership on the \nregional fashion scene. From \n2025 to 2029, Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion and beauty markets \nare forecast to expand by \nUSD 6 billion bringing the \ntotal market value to USD 40 \nbillion.1  2 This growth is driven \nby a combination of factors: a \nrobust economy and thriving \ntourism industry along with \nfavorable demographics \nincluding a young, tech-\nsavvy population with rising \ndisposable incomes. In \nshort, ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-005", "title": "5", "content": "5\nRecent surveys show that \n77% of the public is familiar \nwith Riyadh Fashion Week \n(RFW) and 79% expresses \ninterest in attending future \nRFW shows and events, \nhighlighting the strong \nawareness and cultural \nrelevance of the event.3 \nThis momentum is further \naccelerated by Vision 2030 \ninitiatives, which include \nsubstantial support for \ncreative industries and SMEs \nthrough programs such as the \nSaudi 100 Brands Program \nby the Fashion Commission \nand other programs by the \nCultural Development Fund, \nbolstering both consumer \nconfidence and business \ninnovation across the fashion \necosy", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-006", "title": "601. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  ", "content": "601. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nIn the context of advancing \ndigital technology and Vision \n2030 ambitions, Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion industry has emerged \nas a significant pillar within the \nnation’s diversified economy. \nReflecting broader economic \ntrends, inflation data from the \nEconomist Intelligence Unit \n(EIU) indicates a cumulative \n14% rise in the Consumer Price \nIndex (CPI) between 2019 and \n2024, averaging approximately \n2.7% annually. Additionally, the \nfashion and beauty sectors \nhave exhibited an increased \neconomic multiplier effect, \ndemonstrating higher va", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-007", "title": "7", "content": "7\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nWhile domestic consumption \nremains the primary engine of \ngrowth, the fashion and beauty \nsectors continue to be shaped \nby their integration with \nglobal trade. One illustrative \nexample is apparel imports, \nwhich increased by more \nthan 35% between 2018 and \n2022, rising from USD 3.9 \nbillion to USD 5.4 billion. This \ngrowth was driven by strong \ndemand for international \nbrands, rising household \nspending, and the expansion \nof retail infrastructure. From \n2022 to 2024, however, \napparel imports stabilized at \napproximately USD 5.2 bil", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-008", "title": "801. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  ", "content": "801. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSaudi Arabia’s fashion \nworkforce is an indicator \nof success in meeting \nVision 2030’s social \ntransformation goals, not \nleast when it comes to gender \nand equality. Notably, Saudi \nwomen now comprise of \n55% of the fashion labor \nforce – this underscores \nthe sector’s commitment \nto gender equality and the \nempowerment of women.1\nWomen are not only joining \nthe workforce but also \nrising to leadership roles, \nwith recent statistics \nrevealing that women held \naround 44% of management \npositions in Saudi Arabia last \nyear.7 However, in th", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-009", "title": "9", "content": "9\nSaudi Arabia’s fashion market \nspans a broad spectrum \nfrom affordable mass-market \nproducts to high-end luxury \ngoods, and both segments \nare expanding dynamically. \nThe mass market or non-\nluxury segment, which \nincludes categories like \napparel, footwear, accessories, \nsportwear, beauty, and \njewelry,  makes up the majority \nof spending, accounting for \nroughly 86% of the total \nmarket value. This mainstream \nsegment is expected to reach \na value of approximately \nUSD 28.8 billion in 2025, up from USD 27 .7 billion in \n2024, fueled by a young \npopulation’s need for everyday \nfashion, the ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-010", "title": "10", "content": "10\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSeveral factors are driving this \nupscale growth, including an \nincreasing number of ultra-\nhigh-net-worth individuals. \nThis year, Saudi Arabia is \nhome to nearly 140,700 \nmillionaires. That figure is \nprojected to rise by 85,300 \nby 2030 reaching 226,000, \npositioning the Kingdom as an \nincreasingly attractive market \nfor luxury fashion.2 According \nto Chalhoub Group CEO, \nMichael Chalhoub, while Saudi \nArabia accounted for only 18% \nof GCC personal luxury sales \nin financial year (FY) 2024, it \nis recording growth of +5% year-on- year,", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-011", "title": "11", "content": "11\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSaudi Arabia’s luxury \nmarket was valued at\nUSD 4.6B in 2024 and is \nprojected to reach\nUSD 5.9B by 2029, with \ngrowth led by apparel, \nbeauty, and jewelry \nsegments.\n11FIGURE 3\nLuxury Market Size in Saudi by Product Segment, USD B 1  2Jelwelry and Watches\nBeauty, Personal Care, and Fragrances\nApparel, Footwear, and Accessories\n20224.04.64.34.9\n2023 2024 2025F0.91.0\n1.01.1\n2.4 2.55.9\n... 2029F\n0.9\n0.9\n2.21.1\n1.2\n2.61.3\n1.6\n2.9", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-012", "title": "12The net effect of these ", "content": "12The net effect of these \ndriving forces is balanced \ngrowth across price tiers: \nmass-market fashion is \nsteadily expanding its \nalready large base, while the \nluxury segment is projected \nto grow ~1.2x faster than \nmass fashion.1  2 This dual \nmomentum highlights both \nthe depth and breadth of \nconsumer demand ranging \nfrom value-driven shoppers \nin city malls to luxury \nconnoisseurs at boutique \nopenings. Further reinforcing this trend, \nChalhoub Group’s GCC Luxury \nT racker reveals that over \n50% of Saudi consumers \nfeel financially “very well \noff”, supporting both high-\nticket luxury an", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-013", "title": "13contributed to job creation ", "content": "13contributed to job creation \nand economic diversification, \ntranslating into discretionary \nspending on fashion and \nleisure. In particular, Saudi \nArabia’s middle class and \nupper-middle class segments \nare expanding, producing a \ncadre of consumers eager \nto spend on apparel, beauty, \nand accessories as part of \ntheir lifestyle. Social norms \naround fashion are also evolving. Compared to a \ndecade ago, there is far greater \nacceptance of diverse styles \nand an expectation to express \npersonal identity through \nclothing, particularly among \nthe youth.\nIn short, Saudi Arabia’s  young, \nurban", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-014", "title": "14", "content": "14\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nrise, while inbound tourism \nspending surged to USD \n44.9 billion, up 19% from the \nprevious year. This influx, \ncomprised largely of leisure \ntravelers and religious pilgrims, \nis expected to drive substantial \nspending in retail and fashion, \nfrom luxury goods to everyday \napparel and souvenirs.11\nRetail has become a core \ncomponent of the tourism \nexperience. As new \nattractions such as theme \nparks, beaches, and heritage \nsites are unveiled, they are \nbeing paired with world-class \nshopping complexes and \nconcept stores to capture \nto", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-015", "title": "15", "content": "15\nSaudi Arabia is one of the \nworld’s youngest consumer \nmarkets and its youthful \npopulation is powering \nmomentum within the \nfashion ecosystem. Educated, \ndigitally connected, and \nsocially engaged, young \nSaudis are shaping fashion \nconsumption patterns, and Y outh and Women Driving\nthe Demographic Advantage \nA RESILIENT \nCONSUMER \nBASE: \nDECODING\nAND ENGAGING \nTHE SAUDI \nCONSUMER 02.\ntheir spending power is rising \nas they enter the workforce \nwith increasing propensity. \nNational unemployment in \nfact dropped by around 30% \nbetween 2019 and 2023, with \nthe unemployment rate for the \ntot", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-016", "title": "16As detailed above, women ", "content": "16As detailed above, women \nhave also become formidable \ndrivers of the fashion sector, \nholding more than half of \nthe jobs in the industry and \nassuming management \nand leadership roles across \nsectors. This combined \nforce of youth and female \nempowerment is transforming \nthe Saudi fashion landscape \nfrom both the demand and \nsupply sides.\nCultural pride and social \nmedia influence amplify this \ndemographic advantage. \nY oung Saudis seamlessly fuse 02. A RESILIENT CONSUMER BASE: DECODING AND ENGAGING THE SAUDI CONSUMER \nglobal fashion trends with \nlocal tradition; for instance, \npairing Wes", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-017", "title": "17", "content": "17\nA new 2025 consumer \nperception survey offers \ninsights into purchase \nbehaviors, brand awareness, \nand loyalty across Saudi \nArabia’s diverse shopper \nsegments.\nSpending and Purchase \nBehavior:\nSaudi consumers are \ndemonstrating strong and \nsustained interest in fashion, \nwith 44% reporting an \nincrease in fashion spending \nover the past year and an \nadditional 48% stating that \ntheir spending remained \nconsistent.3 Purchase \nfrequency is also notable: \n43% buy fashion items at \nleast once a month, and 19% \nshop weekly. The key drivers \nbehind increased spending \ninclude the greater availa", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-018", "title": "18", "content": "18\nSaudi consumers seek quality, \nprice competitiveness, \nand national pride when \nsupporting local brands. \nQuality is the top driver, \ninfluencing 85% of \nrespondents, followed closely \nby price competitiveness \n(68%) and the desire to \nsupport local industry (67%). \nDesign/style (55%) and \nsustainability credentials \n(41%) also play a role. Brand \nloyalty is strengthened when \nlabels reflect Saudi culture, \nemploy local creatives, \nand offer exclusivity and \nstorytelling, demonstrating that cultural relevance can \nboost loyalty to both local and \ninternational companies.3 \nIn addition to cu", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-019", "title": "19", "content": "19\nCustomer Experience\nand Satisfaction:\nThe modern Saudi consumer \nis discerning and holds brands \nto high standards. Supporting \nthis point, more than half \nof Saudi consumers (53%) \nidentify product quality as \nthe most important factor \nin their fashion purchase \ndecisions, followed by \nprice and brand reputation. \nBeyond the product itself, \ncustomer experience also \nplays a decisive role, often \noutweighing short-term \ninfluences like social media \ntrends in shaping purchasing \nbehavior.302. A RESILIENT CONSUMER BASE: DECODING AND ENGAGING THE SAUDI CONSUMER \nFIGURE 8\nKey Influencers in ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-020", "title": "20", "content": "20\nT raditional attire remains at \nthe heart of Saudi fashion, \nbut the way consumers \napproach abayas and thobes  \nis evolving with trends \nand personal preferences.\nAbaya Consumption:\nFor Saudi women, the abaya \nis both daily wear and a \nstatement piece. On average, \nSaudi women purchase a \nnew abaya every two to \nthree months, reflecting both \npractical use and seasonal \nfashion cycles. Annual \nspending on abayas averages \naround USD 1,067 (     4,000~) \nper consumer, though this \nvaries widely by income and \ntaste. Affluent fashionistas \nmay invest significantly more \nin designer abayas, w", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-021", "title": "21Thobe Preferences:", "content": "21Thobe Preferences:\nFor Saudi men, the thobe is an \neveryday essential that also \nreflects personal style and \nstatus. Research indicates \nthat men favor tailored thobes \nover ready-to-wear (RTW) \noptions. On average, Saudis \nadd three to four thobes \nannually to their wardrobe, \noften timing purchases before \nEid holidays, weddings, or \nseasonal wardrobe updates. \nThe ratio of tailored to off-\nthe-rack purchases stands at \napproximately 70:30 in favor \nof custom tailoring. In terms \nof spending, Saudis invest \naround \nUSD 667\n(     2,500~) per year on \nthobes, and\nUSD 130–185\n(     500–700 ~", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-022", "title": "22", "content": "22\nOne of the most significant \nshifts in Saudi consumer \nbehavior is the repatriation of \nluxury spending, with affluent \nconsumers increasingly \nchoosing to shop at home \nrather than abroad. Historically, \na large portion of Saudi luxury \nexpenditure leaked to fashion \ncapitals like Dubai, London, \nand Istanbul. For instance, \nduring the last Eid Al Adha, \npremium card spending \nin these cities remained \ndominated by fashion, with \naverage spending per card at \nUSD 399 in Dubai, USD 447 \nin Istanbul, and USD 656 in \nLondon.Repatriation of\nLuxury SpendingSeveral factors underpin this \nshift. ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-023", "title": "23Saudi Arabia’s consumer ", "content": "23Saudi Arabia’s consumer \nbase is not only young and \ntrend conscious; it is also \nbecoming wealthier and more \nconfident. This development  \nis partly driven by successful \neconomic diversification \nefforts under Vision 2030. \nGovernment reforms \nand initiatives have been \nsuccessful in encouraging \nlocal employment, \nentrepreneurship, and the \nincreased participation of \nwomen in the workforce, \ntranslating into higher \ndisposable income for many \nSaudi families.\nLooking ahead, optimism \nis strong among Saudi \nconsumers. Approximately \n44% of consumers in the \nKingdom have increased their \n", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-024", "title": "24", "content": "24\nCAPTURING \nTHE SAUDI \nCONSUMER: \nLOCALIZING \nGLOBAL \nBRANDS, \nELEVA TING \nSAUDI IDENTITY\nSaudi Arabia has cemented its \nposition as a pivotal market \nfor global fashion brands, \ndriven by economic reforms, \na youthful population, and \ngrowing cultural confidence. \nHowever, ‘glocalization’, in one \nway is the ability to balance \nglobal brand equity with \nauthentic local resonance, \nis also key to success. Global \nfashion players are adapting \nby shifting from franchise \nmodels to direct ownership, \nlaunching culturally tailored \ncampaigns, collaborating \nwith Saudi creatives, and \nintegratin", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-025", "title": "25Saudi Arabia’s fashion ", "content": "25Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nlandscape is undergoing a \ntransformation as international \nbrands flood into the market. \nThe Kingdom is fast closing \nthe gap in brand presence, \nwith major luxury houses \nlaunching flagships in Riyadh \nand Jeddah throughout 2024, \nsignaling confidence in Saudi’s \nconsumer base. For example, \nItalian label Dolce & Gabbana \nopened a 16,000-square-foot \nflagship store in the Diriyah \narea, the  brand’s largest one-\nlevel boutique worldwide. The \nsprawling store carries Dolce & \nGabbana’s full collections and \noffers an exclusive collection \nof abayas. These high-profi", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-026", "title": "2603. CAPTURING THE SAUDI CONSUMER: LOCALIZING GLOBAL BRANDS, ELEVA TING SAUDI I", "content": "2603. CAPTURING THE SAUDI CONSUMER: LOCALIZING GLOBAL BRANDS, ELEVA TING SAUDI IDENTITY\nIn response, Saudi Arabia \nhas made starting a business \neasier with streamlined \nlicensing and special economic \nzones that offer tax and \nlogistics benefits. The net \neffect is a steady shift from \nfranchise to direct operations. \nThis trend is expected to \ncontinue as more labels gain \nconfidence in navigating Saudi \nregulations independently.\nMeanwhile, many brands \nare choosing hybrid models \nthat involve entering the \nmarket via JVs with a Saudi \ninvestor or acquiring their \nformer franchisee. These \n", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-027", "title": "27  ", "content": "27  \nFIGURE 11\nT op-of-Mind International Brands Among Saudi Consumers3Apparel\nAthleisure\nFootwear\nAccessories\nBeauty\nCouture\nJewelry & WatchesZara’s vertically integrated \nsupply chain efficiently \nupdates collections twice a \nweek with the latest global \ntrends and regularly introduces \ncapsules for Ramadan and \nEid, ensuring sustained \nrelevance among Saudi \nconsumers. Adidas and Nike \nhave significantly enhanced \ntheir visibility through strategic \ncollaborations, producing \nofficial kits for major Saudi \nfootball clubs including \nAl-Nassr FC and Ittihad FC, \nand actively partnering \nwith ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-028", "title": "28", "content": "28\nT op-of-Mind Saudi Brands: \nA different pattern emerges \nwhen examining Saudi-\norigin brands. Names like \nLomar, Nayomi, T amashee, \nAbdul Samad Al Qurashi, \nand Ashi Studio appear top-\nof-mind across menswear, \nwomenswear, footwear, \nbeauty, and couture segments, \ndemonstrating strong cultural \nresonance and national \naffinity. These brands are \nnot just recognized; they \nare remembered for their \nauthenticity and emotional \nconnection with local \nconsumers.\nNayomi, operating over 103 \nstores across the Kingdom, \nexemplifies widespread \naccessibility and appeal.\nAshi Studio, an internation", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-029", "title": "29A critical pillar enabling ", "content": "29A critical pillar enabling \nthe fashion retail surge is \nSaudi Arabia’s deliberate \nimprovement of its business \nclimate. In recent years, \nauthorities have introduced \nregulatory reforms to make \nmarket entry and operations \neasier for foreign companies. \nOne major reform, although \nnot among the most recent, \nwas the decision to allow \n100% foreign ownership in the \nretail sector, thus eliminating \nthe previous requirement \nfor foreign brands to partner \nwith a local agent or form \na joint venture for store \nownership. Additionally, \nbusiness licensing and permits \nhave been streamlined, w", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-030", "title": "30Amid the rapid growth and ", "content": "30Amid the rapid growth and \nlocalization taking place \nin Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nsector, the voices of a new \ngeneration of fashion students \nand recent graduates are \nincreasingly influential. These \nemerging professionals \nview the landscape as rich \nwith opportunities, blending \nadmiration for the heritage \nand expertise of global luxury \nbrands with a strong aspiration \nto develop or support \nhomegrown brands that \nauthentically represent \nSaudi culture. \nReflecting their ambitions and \nmarket understanding, 75% of \nfuture fashion professionals \nindicate a preference for \ncareers that mi", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-031", "title": "31", "content": "31\nAt the opening ceremony \nof the Paris 2024 Olympics, \nSaudi Arabia’s delegation \nattracted attention not just \nfor its athletic prowess but \nfor the athletes’ attire, which \nexpressed a seamless blend \nof tradition and modernity. \nMale athletes wore the bisht \ncloak over white thobes \nwhile female athletes \nwore Al-Nashal dresses, all \nembellished with motifs \nreflecting Saudi Arabia’s \nancient history. The outfits, \ndesigned by Saudi creative \nAlia Al-Salmi, touched a global \naudience and became CUL TURAL \nHERIT AGE:\nTHE FUTURE\nOF SAUDI\nSTYLE04.a symbol of cultural pride. This \nstriking mo", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-032", "title": "32Heritage crafts are at the ", "content": "32Heritage crafts are at the \nheart of Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nrevival. Attesting to their \nimportance, Saudi Arabia \nhas officially declared 2025 \nthe ‘Y ear of Handicrafts’ \nin celebration of its rich \ncraftsmanship heritage. This \nsignificant move shines a \nspotlight on age-old artisanal \nskills from Al-Sadu weaving \nto Al-Qatt Al-Asiri patterns, \nhighlighting them not as relics \nof the past, but as living arts. \nThe domestic handicrafts \nindustry is projected to reach \nUSD 1.35 billion by 2028.15\nAcross Saudi Arabia, this \nrevival is actively supported \nby 40 professional crafts \nassociati", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-033", "title": "33", "content": "33\nSaudi Arabia’s unique take \non fashion is being led by \na dynamic cohort of Saudi \ndesigners who are giving \nfamiliar ready-to-wear \ngarments a heritage-infused \nupdate. Designers have \nreimagined the classic \nabaya with bold colors and \ninnovative cuts and have \ncrafted menswear that pays \ntribute to traditional attire like \nthe thobe and bisht in fresh, \nurbane contexts. Blending Past\nand Present04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nThis blending of past and \npresent is not just an aesthetic \nchoice; it is a statement of \nSaudi Arabia’s evolving identity \non the global fashio", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-034", "title": "34", "content": "34\nReflecting on the growing \nsuccess of local brands, \nSaudi fashion designer, Reem \nAlkanhal, points out how far \nthings have come. “A decade \nago, there were few of us, \nlimited infrastructure, and \nlocal options. T oday, there’s \na real ecosystem emerging, \none that’s proudly Saudi,” she \nsays. Alkanhal’s own work \nembodies this transformation. \nKnown for her minimalist \nyet evocative collections, \nshe reinterprets traditional \nSaudi elements into fluid, \ncontemporary silhouettes \ndesigned to resonate globally. \nIn her celebrated Sword \nCollection, she distills cultural \nheritage into clea", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-035", "title": "35Other industry trailblazers are ", "content": "35Other industry trailblazers are \nalso bringing Saudi heritage \nto world platforms. Luxury \napparel designer, Hindamme, \nincorporated Arabic \ncalligraphy and references \nto historical events into his \njackets, and Arwa Al Banawi \ninfuses streetwear with Arabic \ntypography and Bedouin \npatterns. Such designers \nembody a new confidence \namong Saudi creatives, \nreinforcing the message that \ntraditions are not hindrances \nbut rich inspiration for \ninnovation.04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nThere is a business case for \nSaudi Arabia’s fusion of cultural \nheritage with modern fas", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-036", "title": "36The imprint of Saudi heritage ", "content": "36The imprint of Saudi heritage \nextends beyond clothing, \nshaping Saudi Arabia’s broader \ncreative and consumer \nlandscape. In beauty and \nlifestyle, Saudi entrepreneurs \nare preserving tradition \nwhile adapting it for modern \naudiences. For example, oud, \nthe deeply aromatic wood \nlong cherished in the region, \nremains central to local \nfragrance identity. Its use in \nhigh-end perfumes continues \nto reflect Saudi Arabia’s \ndeep-rooted appreciation for \nsensory traditions, while also \nreinforcing the nation’s cultural \ndistinctiveness in the global \nluxury market.A Cultural\nRenaissance in\nBea", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-037", "title": "37The convergence of cultural ", "content": "37The convergence of cultural \nheritage and modern fashion \nis expected to grow stronger \nthroughout 2025. Already \nthis year, Saudi Arabia \nhas participated in Expo \n2025 Osaka, showcasing \nits heritage at a pavilion \nactivation titled ‘Handicraft \nExperiences: A Celebration \nof Art and Innovation.’ The \nweek-long exhibit in Japan \ndrew thousands of visitors \nand put Saudi identity in the \nspotlight through modern \ninterpretations of traditional \ncrafts. Among the designs Crafting Global\nNarratives from\nLocal Traditions04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nand innovations on show", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-038", "title": "38", "content": "38\nOMNI-CHANNEL \nAND DIGIT AL \nENGAGEMENT\nAs Saudi Arabia positions \nitself as an innovator in \nfashion, the sector is \nundergoing profound digital \ntransformation in line with \nVision 2030’s drive to \nmodernize the economy. With \na connected and tech-savvy \nconsumer base, this evolution \nis a natural progression, but \nit meets head on with Saudi \nArabia’s enduring passion \nfor in-person shopping. This \ndynamic has given rise to \nomni-channel strategies \nthat blend online and offline \ntouchpoints as brands adapt \nto meet Saudi shoppers \nwherever they are. \nFrom surging e-commerce \nadoption to ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-039", "title": "39The Saudi government’s ", "content": "39The Saudi government’s \npush for a digital economy \nhas set the stage for robust \ne-commerce expansion. \nInternet penetration has \nreached 99% in Saudi Arabia, \nand nearly all adults use \nsmartphones, creating a \nready base for online retail. \nIn fashion, online sales have \nskyrocketed.18 Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion e-commerce market \nwas valued at approximately E-Commerce Is\nRising, But not at the \nExpense of Stores05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nand is projected to exceed USD \n7 .6 billion by 2029.19 Half of \nrespondents reported buying \nmost of their fashion online, \nwhile 35% said t", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-040", "title": "40Despite the rise of ", "content": "40Despite the rise of \ne-commerce, Saudi consumers \nremain deeply connected \nto mall culture. In a country \nwhere malls serve as social \nhubs and leisure destinations, \nshopping is more than a \ntransaction, it is a lifestyle and \nform of entertainment. This \ncultural preference ensures \nthat physical retail not only \nendures, but continues \nto thrive alongside online \nshopping.05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nThe strength of Saudi mall \nculture has made the retail \nsector relatively resilient to \nthe e-commerce onslaught \nseen elsewhere. Saudi mall \noperators note that local \ncustomers", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-041", "title": "41Why Omni-Channel, ", "content": "41Why Omni-Channel, \nWhy Now?05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nSaudi shoppers comfortably \nuse multiple channels. They \nexpect a seamless journey \nwhether they are scrolling \nthrough a mobile app at \nhome or browsing in-store \nat their favorite shopping \ndestination. Ubiquitous digital \nadoption is a major factor in \nthis development. In 2024, \n79% of all retail transactions \nacross sectors involved \ncashless electronic payments, \nindicating comfort with digital \ncommerce infrastructure.5 In \naddition, consumer behavior \noften blends channels: a \ncustomer might discover a \nfashion item ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-042", "title": "4205. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT", "content": "4205. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\n42Across the Kingdom, there \nis a common pattern of \nconsumers carrying out \nonline research before \nbuying in a physical store, \nand retailers have responded \nby ensuring their online \nchannels complement the \nstore experience. For example, \nmany retailers enable real-\ntime inventory checks in \nnearby stores or offer click-\nand-collect services and easy \nin-store returns for online \npurchases. Over the past year, \nseveral leading fashion chains \nin Saudi Arabia have rolled out \nclick-and-collect at dozens of \nbranches, recognizing that \nmany shoppers", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-043", "title": "43", "content": "43\nOn Saudi Arabia’s omni-\nchannel landscape, social \nmedia plays an outsized role \nin fashion engagement. The \nKingdom boasts one of the \nworld’s most active social \nmedia populations, particularly \nwhen it comes to Gen Z, with \nplatforms like Instagram, \nSnapchat, TikT ok, and X acting \nas virtual runways and market \nforums that heavily influence \npurchase decisions. T oday, \nyoung consumers scroll \nthrough influencer posts, \nbrand lookbooks, and style \nvideos to discover the latest \ntrends. A strong majority \n(81%) of survey respondents \nsay it is important that brands \nreflect the latest f", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-044", "title": "44FIGURE 20", "content": "44FIGURE 20\nT op Discovery Channels for New Beauty Products Among Saudi Consumers (%)3TikT ok is the top discovery \nplatform for new beauty \nproducts at 51%, followed \nby peer recommendations \n(43%) and Instagram (40%), \nconfirming the powerful \nrole of short-form video \nand social trust in shaping \nbeauty awareness and \npurchase intent.\nInfluencers as Key \nIntermediaries:\nWith social media acting as \na powerful platform, Saudi \nArabia’s fashion influencer \neconomy has grown into a \ndynamic industry. Hundreds \nof influencers now serve \nas trendsetters and brand \nambassadors with sway \nover Gen", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-045", "title": "45FIGURE 20", "content": "45FIGURE 20\nT op Discovery Channels for New Beauty Products Among Saudi Consumers (%)3\nPersonalization\nacross channels: \nOmni-channel engagement \nin Saudi Arabia is increasingly \npowered by personalization, \nespecially for younger and \naffluent consumers. Retailers \nuse loyalty data and insights \ninto online behavior to \ncustomize digital and in-store \nexperiences. Mobile apps \nhighlight relevant styles, like \nfashion tailored for abaya Blending Online and \nOffline: Omni-Channel \nStrategies in Action05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nbuyers or streetwear for Gen \nZ men, while in store, l", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-046", "title": "46", "content": "46\n further trend that straddles \nthe online and offline worlds \nis concept stores that blend \ncurated, lifestyle-driven \nshopping with community \nand social media appeal. In \nSaudi Arabia, concept stores \noften feature local designers, \nthemed decor, cafés, and art \ncorners, creating immersive \ncustomer experiences. Their \nhybrid nature, which is part \nboutique, part cultural hub, Concept Stores\nand Immersive\nExperiences05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\ncombines physical retail \nwith digital engagement and \ncommunity-building. \nThe scene is rapidly expanding, \nparticularly in Riyadh an", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-047", "title": "47", "content": "47\nROBUST RET AIL LANDSCAPE\nAND INFRASTRUCTURE:\nTHE KEY ROLE OF MALLS  06.\nThe previous edition of The \nState of Fashion Sector in \nSaudi Arabia report (2024) \nhighlighted how Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion market was deeply \nshaped by its mall-driven retail \ninfrastructure. Last year, over \nhalf of the nation’s fashion \nspending was concentrated \nin the three cities of Riyadh, \nJeddah, and Dammam, with \nmalls serving not only as \ncommercial hubs but also \nas key lifestyle and social \ndestinations. As stated in the \n2024 report, the top five mall \noperators (Cenomi, Al Othaim, \nHamat, Alandalus, and ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-048", "title": "48Public and private ", "content": "48Public and private \ndevelopers in Saudi Arabia \nare aggressively building \nnew malls to boost capacity \nand quality, with organized \nretail gross leasable area \n(GLA) in major cities on track \nto double by 2030, fueled by \ngiga-projects and modern \nshopping centers. New \ndevelopments aim to surpass \nexisting regional benchmarks, \nfeaturing cutting-edge \narchitecture, better traffic flow \nand parking access, and an \nelevated tenant mix. As a result \nof these efforts, financiers \nand investors are gaining \nconfidence; sentiment around \nfunding large shopping centers \nhas improved, as strong \nd", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-049", "title": "49", "content": "49\nMalls must cater to a broad \nspectrum of visitors from \nvalue-focused families to \nultra-high-net-worth shoppers. \nAs such, there is a need to \nbalance mass-market and \nluxury retail under one roof. \nIn practice, this means that \nlarge ‘power malls’ anchor \nfast fashion chains, mid-range \nbrands, and department \nstores in high-traffic areas, \nwhile carving out exclusive \nwings or sections for luxury \nboutiques. Mass-market \nretail dominates in terms \nof floor space, footfall, and \nsales volume, with top mall \noperators like Cenomi and \nAl Othaim emphasizing \nglobal fast fashion brands \nand ", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_FC_Report_20-050", "title": "50A deeper dive into mall ", "content": "50A deeper dive into mall \ndynamics uncovers interesting \npatterns in terms of product \ncategory share and retail \nformats: \nFragrance and Beauty:\nBeauty and personal \ncare accounts for a \ndisproportionate share of \nSaudi retail, with the beauty/\nfragrance category often \nrivaling apparel in count of \nstores. This reflects consumer \nspending patterns; Saudis \noften spend generously on \nperfumes, cosmetics, and \npersonal grooming, making \nthe category a key revenue \ndriver. Culturally, fragrances \nare particularly highly prized. \nMall corridors are filled with \nperfume boutiques, including \na m", "source": "market/Digital_FC_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-001", "title": "1THE ", "content": "1THE \nST A TE OF \nFASHION \nSECTOR \nIN SAUDI \nARABIA \n2025", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-002", "title": "2FOREWORD", "content": "2FOREWORD\nCEO Message\n1. Saudi Arabia: A Strong and Growing Fashion Hub\nDEMAND\n2. A Resilient Consumer Base: Decoding and Engaging the Saudi Consumer\n3. Capturing the Saudi Consumer: Localizing Global Brands, Elevating Saudi Identity\n4. Cultural Heritage: The Future of Saudi Style\n5. Omni-Channel and Digital Engagement\nSUPPL Y\n6. Robust Retail Landscape and Infrastructure: The Key Role of Malls\n7. Building Local Capacity: Manufacturing and Skills\n8. Sustainability and Circular Economy: Local Actions, Global Impact\nDEEP DIVE\n9. Riyadh Fashion Week: A New Driver of Saudi T ourism\n10. Wellness, L", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-003", "title": "3", "content": "3\nCEO MESSAGE \nMarking five years since the founding of the Saudi Fashion Commission \ninvites both reflection and anticipation. What began as a mandate to build the \nfoundations of a national fashion sector has evolved into a broader mission \nto shape a creative economy rooted in identity and resilience. This year, the \nfashion industry in Saudi Arabia is expected to reach USD 34 billion, reaffirming \nthe Kingdom’s position as the fastest growing market in the GCC.\nY et the numbers only tell part of the story. What they reflect is the energy of a \nnew generation of designers and entrepreneurs,", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-004", "title": "4", "content": "4\nSAUDI ARABIA: \nA STRONG \nAND GROWING \nFASHION HUB\nSaudi Arabia’s fashion \nmarket continues to surge \nahead, experiencing rapid \ngrowth that is solidifying the \nKingdom’s leadership on the \nregional fashion scene. From \n2025 to 2029, Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion and beauty markets \nare forecast to expand by \nUSD 6 billion bringing the \ntotal market value to USD 40 \nbillion.1  2 This growth is driven \nby a combination of factors: a \nrobust economy and thriving \ntourism industry along with \nfavorable demographics \nincluding a young, tech-\nsavvy population with rising \ndisposable incomes. In \nshort, ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-005", "title": "5", "content": "5\nRecent surveys show that \n77% of the public is familiar \nwith Riyadh Fashion Week \n(RFW) and 79% expresses \ninterest in attending future \nRFW shows and events, \nhighlighting the strong \nawareness and cultural \nrelevance of the event.3 \nThis momentum is further \naccelerated by Vision 2030 \ninitiatives, which include \nsubstantial support for \ncreative industries and SMEs \nthrough programs such as the \nSaudi 100 Brands Program \nby the Fashion Commission \nand other programs by the \nCultural Development Fund, \nbolstering both consumer \nconfidence and business \ninnovation across the fashion \necosy", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-006", "title": "601. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  ", "content": "601. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nIn the context of advancing \ndigital technology and Vision \n2030 ambitions, Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion industry has emerged \nas a significant pillar within the \nnation’s diversified economy. \nReflecting broader economic \ntrends, inflation data from the \nEconomist Intelligence Unit \n(EIU) indicates a cumulative \n14% rise in the Consumer Price \nIndex (CPI) between 2019 and \n2024, averaging approximately \n2.7% annually. Additionally, the \nfashion and beauty sectors \nhave exhibited an increased \neconomic multiplier effect, \ndemonstrating higher va", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-007", "title": "7", "content": "7\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nWhile domestic consumption \nremains the primary engine of \ngrowth, the fashion and beauty \nsectors continue to be shaped \nby their integration with \nglobal trade. One illustrative \nexample is apparel imports, \nwhich increased by more \nthan 35% between 2018 and \n2022, rising from USD 3.9 \nbillion to USD 5.4 billion. This \ngrowth was driven by strong \ndemand for international \nbrands, rising household \nspending, and the expansion \nof retail infrastructure. From \n2022 to 2024, however, \napparel imports stabilized at \napproximately USD 5.2 bil", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-008", "title": "801. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  ", "content": "801. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSaudi Arabia’s fashion \nworkforce is an indicator \nof success in meeting \nVision 2030’s social \ntransformation goals, not \nleast when it comes to gender \nand equality. Notably, Saudi \nwomen now comprise of \n55% of the fashion labor \nforce – this underscores \nthe sector’s commitment \nto gender equality and the \nempowerment of women.1\nWomen are not only joining \nthe workforce but also \nrising to leadership roles, \nwith recent statistics \nrevealing that women held \naround 44% of management \npositions in Saudi Arabia last \nyear.7 However, in th", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-009", "title": "9", "content": "9\nSaudi Arabia’s fashion market \nspans a broad spectrum \nfrom affordable mass-market \nproducts to high-end luxury \ngoods, and both segments \nare expanding dynamically. \nThe mass market or non-\nluxury segment, which \nincludes categories like \napparel, footwear, accessories, \nsportwear, beauty, and \njewelry,  makes up the majority \nof spending, accounting for \nroughly 86% of the total \nmarket value. This mainstream \nsegment is expected to reach \na value of approximately \nUSD 28.8 billion in 2025, up from USD 27 .7 billion in \n2024, fueled by a young \npopulation’s need for everyday \nfashion, the ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-010", "title": "10", "content": "10\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSeveral factors are driving this \nupscale growth, including an \nincreasing number of ultra-\nhigh-net-worth individuals. \nThis year, Saudi Arabia is \nhome to nearly 140,700 \nmillionaires. That figure is \nprojected to rise by 85,300 \nby 2030 reaching 226,000, \npositioning the Kingdom as an \nincreasingly attractive market \nfor luxury fashion.2 According \nto Chalhoub Group CEO, \nMichael Chalhoub, while Saudi \nArabia accounted for only 18% \nof GCC personal luxury sales \nin financial year (FY) 2024, it \nis recording growth of +5% year-on- year,", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-011", "title": "11", "content": "11\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nSaudi Arabia’s luxury \nmarket was valued at\nUSD 4.6B in 2024 and is \nprojected to reach\nUSD 5.9B by 2029, with \ngrowth led by apparel, \nbeauty, and jewelry \nsegments.\n11FIGURE 3\nLuxury Market Size in Saudi by Product Segment, USD B 1  2Jelwelry and Watches\nBeauty, Personal Care, and Fragrances\nApparel, Footwear, and Accessories\n20224.04.64.34.9\n2023 2024 2025F0.91.0\n1.01.1\n2.4 2.55.9\n... 2029F\n0.9\n0.9\n2.21.1\n1.2\n2.61.3\n1.6\n2.9", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-012", "title": "12The net effect of these ", "content": "12The net effect of these \ndriving forces is balanced \ngrowth across price tiers: \nmass-market fashion is \nsteadily expanding its \nalready large base, while the \nluxury segment is projected \nto grow ~1.2x faster than \nmass fashion.1  2 This dual \nmomentum highlights both \nthe depth and breadth of \nconsumer demand ranging \nfrom value-driven shoppers \nin city malls to luxury \nconnoisseurs at boutique \nopenings. Further reinforcing this trend, \nChalhoub Group’s GCC Luxury \nT racker reveals that over \n50% of Saudi consumers \nfeel financially “very well \noff”, supporting both high-\nticket luxury an", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-013", "title": "13contributed to job creation ", "content": "13contributed to job creation \nand economic diversification, \ntranslating into discretionary \nspending on fashion and \nleisure. In particular, Saudi \nArabia’s middle class and \nupper-middle class segments \nare expanding, producing a \ncadre of consumers eager \nto spend on apparel, beauty, \nand accessories as part of \ntheir lifestyle. Social norms \naround fashion are also evolving. Compared to a \ndecade ago, there is far greater \nacceptance of diverse styles \nand an expectation to express \npersonal identity through \nclothing, particularly among \nthe youth.\nIn short, Saudi Arabia’s  young, \nurban", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-014", "title": "14", "content": "14\n01. SAUDI ARABIA: A STRONG AND GROWING FASHION HUB  \nrise, while inbound tourism \nspending surged to USD \n44.9 billion, up 19% from the \nprevious year. This influx, \ncomprised largely of leisure \ntravelers and religious pilgrims, \nis expected to drive substantial \nspending in retail and fashion, \nfrom luxury goods to everyday \napparel and souvenirs.11\nRetail has become a core \ncomponent of the tourism \nexperience. As new \nattractions such as theme \nparks, beaches, and heritage \nsites are unveiled, they are \nbeing paired with world-class \nshopping complexes and \nconcept stores to capture \nto", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-015", "title": "15", "content": "15\nSaudi Arabia is one of the \nworld’s youngest consumer \nmarkets and its youthful \npopulation is powering \nmomentum within the \nfashion ecosystem. Educated, \ndigitally connected, and \nsocially engaged, young \nSaudis are shaping fashion \nconsumption patterns, and Y outh and Women Driving\nthe Demographic Advantage \nA RESILIENT \nCONSUMER \nBASE: \nDECODING\nAND ENGAGING \nTHE SAUDI \nCONSUMER 02.\ntheir spending power is rising \nas they enter the workforce \nwith increasing propensity. \nNational unemployment in \nfact dropped by around 30% \nbetween 2019 and 2023, with \nthe unemployment rate for the \ntot", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-016", "title": "16As detailed above, women ", "content": "16As detailed above, women \nhave also become formidable \ndrivers of the fashion sector, \nholding more than half of \nthe jobs in the industry and \nassuming management \nand leadership roles across \nsectors. This combined \nforce of youth and female \nempowerment is transforming \nthe Saudi fashion landscape \nfrom both the demand and \nsupply sides.\nCultural pride and social \nmedia influence amplify this \ndemographic advantage. \nY oung Saudis seamlessly fuse 02. A RESILIENT CONSUMER BASE: DECODING AND ENGAGING THE SAUDI CONSUMER \nglobal fashion trends with \nlocal tradition; for instance, \npairing Wes", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-017", "title": "17", "content": "17\nA new 2025 consumer \nperception survey offers \ninsights into purchase \nbehaviors, brand awareness, \nand loyalty across Saudi \nArabia’s diverse shopper \nsegments.\nSpending and Purchase \nBehavior:\nSaudi consumers are \ndemonstrating strong and \nsustained interest in fashion, \nwith 44% reporting an \nincrease in fashion spending \nover the past year and an \nadditional 48% stating that \ntheir spending remained \nconsistent.3 Purchase \nfrequency is also notable: \n43% buy fashion items at \nleast once a month, and 19% \nshop weekly. The key drivers \nbehind increased spending \ninclude the greater availa", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-018", "title": "18", "content": "18\nSaudi consumers seek quality, \nprice competitiveness, \nand national pride when \nsupporting local brands. \nQuality is the top driver, \ninfluencing 85% of \nrespondents, followed closely \nby price competitiveness \n(68%) and the desire to \nsupport local industry (67%). \nDesign/style (55%) and \nsustainability credentials \n(41%) also play a role. Brand \nloyalty is strengthened when \nlabels reflect Saudi culture, \nemploy local creatives, \nand offer exclusivity and \nstorytelling, demonstrating that cultural relevance can \nboost loyalty to both local and \ninternational companies.3 \nIn addition to cu", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-019", "title": "19", "content": "19\nCustomer Experience\nand Satisfaction:\nThe modern Saudi consumer \nis discerning and holds brands \nto high standards. Supporting \nthis point, more than half \nof Saudi consumers (53%) \nidentify product quality as \nthe most important factor \nin their fashion purchase \ndecisions, followed by \nprice and brand reputation. \nBeyond the product itself, \ncustomer experience also \nplays a decisive role, often \noutweighing short-term \ninfluences like social media \ntrends in shaping purchasing \nbehavior.302. A RESILIENT CONSUMER BASE: DECODING AND ENGAGING THE SAUDI CONSUMER \nFIGURE 8\nKey Influencers in ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-020", "title": "20", "content": "20\nT raditional attire remains at \nthe heart of Saudi fashion, \nbut the way consumers \napproach abayas and thobes  \nis evolving with trends \nand personal preferences.\nAbaya Consumption:\nFor Saudi women, the abaya \nis both daily wear and a \nstatement piece. On average, \nSaudi women purchase a \nnew abaya every two to \nthree months, reflecting both \npractical use and seasonal \nfashion cycles. Annual \nspending on abayas averages \naround USD 1,067 (     4,000~) \nper consumer, though this \nvaries widely by income and \ntaste. Affluent fashionistas \nmay invest significantly more \nin designer abayas, w", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-021", "title": "21Thobe Preferences:", "content": "21Thobe Preferences:\nFor Saudi men, the thobe is an \neveryday essential that also \nreflects personal style and \nstatus. Research indicates \nthat men favor tailored thobes \nover ready-to-wear (RTW) \noptions. On average, Saudis \nadd three to four thobes \nannually to their wardrobe, \noften timing purchases before \nEid holidays, weddings, or \nseasonal wardrobe updates. \nThe ratio of tailored to off-\nthe-rack purchases stands at \napproximately 70:30 in favor \nof custom tailoring. In terms \nof spending, Saudis invest \naround \nUSD 667\n(     2,500~) per year on \nthobes, and\nUSD 130–185\n(     500–700 ~", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-022", "title": "22", "content": "22\nOne of the most significant \nshifts in Saudi consumer \nbehavior is the repatriation of \nluxury spending, with affluent \nconsumers increasingly \nchoosing to shop at home \nrather than abroad. Historically, \na large portion of Saudi luxury \nexpenditure leaked to fashion \ncapitals like Dubai, London, \nand Istanbul. For instance, \nduring the last Eid Al Adha, \npremium card spending \nin these cities remained \ndominated by fashion, with \naverage spending per card at \nUSD 399 in Dubai, USD 447 \nin Istanbul, and USD 656 in \nLondon.Repatriation of\nLuxury SpendingSeveral factors underpin this \nshift. ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-023", "title": "23Saudi Arabia’s consumer ", "content": "23Saudi Arabia’s consumer \nbase is not only young and \ntrend conscious; it is also \nbecoming wealthier and more \nconfident. This development  \nis partly driven by successful \neconomic diversification \nefforts under Vision 2030. \nGovernment reforms \nand initiatives have been \nsuccessful in encouraging \nlocal employment, \nentrepreneurship, and the \nincreased participation of \nwomen in the workforce, \ntranslating into higher \ndisposable income for many \nSaudi families.\nLooking ahead, optimism \nis strong among Saudi \nconsumers. Approximately \n44% of consumers in the \nKingdom have increased their \n", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-024", "title": "24", "content": "24\nCAPTURING \nTHE SAUDI \nCONSUMER: \nLOCALIZING \nGLOBAL \nBRANDS, \nELEVA TING \nSAUDI IDENTITY\nSaudi Arabia has cemented its \nposition as a pivotal market \nfor global fashion brands, \ndriven by economic reforms, \na youthful population, and \ngrowing cultural confidence. \nHowever, ‘glocalization’, in one \nway is the ability to balance \nglobal brand equity with \nauthentic local resonance, \nis also key to success. Global \nfashion players are adapting \nby shifting from franchise \nmodels to direct ownership, \nlaunching culturally tailored \ncampaigns, collaborating \nwith Saudi creatives, and \nintegratin", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-025", "title": "25Saudi Arabia’s fashion ", "content": "25Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nlandscape is undergoing a \ntransformation as international \nbrands flood into the market. \nThe Kingdom is fast closing \nthe gap in brand presence, \nwith major luxury houses \nlaunching flagships in Riyadh \nand Jeddah throughout 2024, \nsignaling confidence in Saudi’s \nconsumer base. For example, \nItalian label Dolce & Gabbana \nopened a 16,000-square-foot \nflagship store in the Diriyah \narea, the  brand’s largest one-\nlevel boutique worldwide. The \nsprawling store carries Dolce & \nGabbana’s full collections and \noffers an exclusive collection \nof abayas. These high-profi", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-026", "title": "2603. CAPTURING THE SAUDI CONSUMER: LOCALIZING GLOBAL BRANDS, ELEVA TING SAUDI I", "content": "2603. CAPTURING THE SAUDI CONSUMER: LOCALIZING GLOBAL BRANDS, ELEVA TING SAUDI IDENTITY\nIn response, Saudi Arabia \nhas made starting a business \neasier with streamlined \nlicensing and special economic \nzones that offer tax and \nlogistics benefits. The net \neffect is a steady shift from \nfranchise to direct operations. \nThis trend is expected to \ncontinue as more labels gain \nconfidence in navigating Saudi \nregulations independently.\nMeanwhile, many brands \nare choosing hybrid models \nthat involve entering the \nmarket via JVs with a Saudi \ninvestor or acquiring their \nformer franchisee. These \n", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-027", "title": "27  ", "content": "27  \nFIGURE 11\nT op-of-Mind International Brands Among Saudi Consumers3Apparel\nAthleisure\nFootwear\nAccessories\nBeauty\nCouture\nJewelry & WatchesZara’s vertically integrated \nsupply chain efficiently \nupdates collections twice a \nweek with the latest global \ntrends and regularly introduces \ncapsules for Ramadan and \nEid, ensuring sustained \nrelevance among Saudi \nconsumers. Adidas and Nike \nhave significantly enhanced \ntheir visibility through strategic \ncollaborations, producing \nofficial kits for major Saudi \nfootball clubs including \nAl-Nassr FC and Ittihad FC, \nand actively partnering \nwith ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-028", "title": "28", "content": "28\nT op-of-Mind Saudi Brands: \nA different pattern emerges \nwhen examining Saudi-\norigin brands. Names like \nLomar, Nayomi, T amashee, \nAbdul Samad Al Qurashi, \nand Ashi Studio appear top-\nof-mind across menswear, \nwomenswear, footwear, \nbeauty, and couture segments, \ndemonstrating strong cultural \nresonance and national \naffinity. These brands are \nnot just recognized; they \nare remembered for their \nauthenticity and emotional \nconnection with local \nconsumers.\nNayomi, operating over 103 \nstores across the Kingdom, \nexemplifies widespread \naccessibility and appeal.\nAshi Studio, an internation", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-029", "title": "29A critical pillar enabling ", "content": "29A critical pillar enabling \nthe fashion retail surge is \nSaudi Arabia’s deliberate \nimprovement of its business \nclimate. In recent years, \nauthorities have introduced \nregulatory reforms to make \nmarket entry and operations \neasier for foreign companies. \nOne major reform, although \nnot among the most recent, \nwas the decision to allow \n100% foreign ownership in the \nretail sector, thus eliminating \nthe previous requirement \nfor foreign brands to partner \nwith a local agent or form \na joint venture for store \nownership. Additionally, \nbusiness licensing and permits \nhave been streamlined, w", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-030", "title": "30Amid the rapid growth and ", "content": "30Amid the rapid growth and \nlocalization taking place \nin Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nsector, the voices of a new \ngeneration of fashion students \nand recent graduates are \nincreasingly influential. These \nemerging professionals \nview the landscape as rich \nwith opportunities, blending \nadmiration for the heritage \nand expertise of global luxury \nbrands with a strong aspiration \nto develop or support \nhomegrown brands that \nauthentically represent \nSaudi culture. \nReflecting their ambitions and \nmarket understanding, 75% of \nfuture fashion professionals \nindicate a preference for \ncareers that mi", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-031", "title": "31", "content": "31\nAt the opening ceremony \nof the Paris 2024 Olympics, \nSaudi Arabia’s delegation \nattracted attention not just \nfor its athletic prowess but \nfor the athletes’ attire, which \nexpressed a seamless blend \nof tradition and modernity. \nMale athletes wore the bisht \ncloak over white thobes \nwhile female athletes \nwore Al-Nashal dresses, all \nembellished with motifs \nreflecting Saudi Arabia’s \nancient history. The outfits, \ndesigned by Saudi creative \nAlia Al-Salmi, touched a global \naudience and became CUL TURAL \nHERIT AGE:\nTHE FUTURE\nOF SAUDI\nSTYLE04.a symbol of cultural pride. This \nstriking mo", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-032", "title": "32Heritage crafts are at the ", "content": "32Heritage crafts are at the \nheart of Saudi Arabia’s fashion \nrevival. Attesting to their \nimportance, Saudi Arabia \nhas officially declared 2025 \nthe ‘Y ear of Handicrafts’ \nin celebration of its rich \ncraftsmanship heritage. This \nsignificant move shines a \nspotlight on age-old artisanal \nskills from Al-Sadu weaving \nto Al-Qatt Al-Asiri patterns, \nhighlighting them not as relics \nof the past, but as living arts. \nThe domestic handicrafts \nindustry is projected to reach \nUSD 1.35 billion by 2028.15\nAcross Saudi Arabia, this \nrevival is actively supported \nby 40 professional crafts \nassociati", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-033", "title": "33", "content": "33\nSaudi Arabia’s unique take \non fashion is being led by \na dynamic cohort of Saudi \ndesigners who are giving \nfamiliar ready-to-wear \ngarments a heritage-infused \nupdate. Designers have \nreimagined the classic \nabaya with bold colors and \ninnovative cuts and have \ncrafted menswear that pays \ntribute to traditional attire like \nthe thobe and bisht in fresh, \nurbane contexts. Blending Past\nand Present04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nThis blending of past and \npresent is not just an aesthetic \nchoice; it is a statement of \nSaudi Arabia’s evolving identity \non the global fashio", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-034", "title": "34", "content": "34\nReflecting on the growing \nsuccess of local brands, \nSaudi fashion designer, Reem \nAlkanhal, points out how far \nthings have come. “A decade \nago, there were few of us, \nlimited infrastructure, and \nlocal options. T oday, there’s \na real ecosystem emerging, \none that’s proudly Saudi,” she \nsays. Alkanhal’s own work \nembodies this transformation. \nKnown for her minimalist \nyet evocative collections, \nshe reinterprets traditional \nSaudi elements into fluid, \ncontemporary silhouettes \ndesigned to resonate globally. \nIn her celebrated Sword \nCollection, she distills cultural \nheritage into clea", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-035", "title": "35Other industry trailblazers are ", "content": "35Other industry trailblazers are \nalso bringing Saudi heritage \nto world platforms. Luxury \napparel designer, Hindamme, \nincorporated Arabic \ncalligraphy and references \nto historical events into his \njackets, and Arwa Al Banawi \ninfuses streetwear with Arabic \ntypography and Bedouin \npatterns. Such designers \nembody a new confidence \namong Saudi creatives, \nreinforcing the message that \ntraditions are not hindrances \nbut rich inspiration for \ninnovation.04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nThere is a business case for \nSaudi Arabia’s fusion of cultural \nheritage with modern fas", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-036", "title": "36The imprint of Saudi heritage ", "content": "36The imprint of Saudi heritage \nextends beyond clothing, \nshaping Saudi Arabia’s broader \ncreative and consumer \nlandscape. In beauty and \nlifestyle, Saudi entrepreneurs \nare preserving tradition \nwhile adapting it for modern \naudiences. For example, oud, \nthe deeply aromatic wood \nlong cherished in the region, \nremains central to local \nfragrance identity. Its use in \nhigh-end perfumes continues \nto reflect Saudi Arabia’s \ndeep-rooted appreciation for \nsensory traditions, while also \nreinforcing the nation’s cultural \ndistinctiveness in the global \nluxury market.A Cultural\nRenaissance in\nBea", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-037", "title": "37The convergence of cultural ", "content": "37The convergence of cultural \nheritage and modern fashion \nis expected to grow stronger \nthroughout 2025. Already \nthis year, Saudi Arabia \nhas participated in Expo \n2025 Osaka, showcasing \nits heritage at a pavilion \nactivation titled ‘Handicraft \nExperiences: A Celebration \nof Art and Innovation.’ The \nweek-long exhibit in Japan \ndrew thousands of visitors \nand put Saudi identity in the \nspotlight through modern \ninterpretations of traditional \ncrafts. Among the designs Crafting Global\nNarratives from\nLocal Traditions04. CUL TURAL HERIT AGE: THE FUTURE OF SAUDI STYLE\nand innovations on show", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-038", "title": "38", "content": "38\nOMNI-CHANNEL \nAND DIGIT AL \nENGAGEMENT\nAs Saudi Arabia positions \nitself as an innovator in \nfashion, the sector is \nundergoing profound digital \ntransformation in line with \nVision 2030’s drive to \nmodernize the economy. With \na connected and tech-savvy \nconsumer base, this evolution \nis a natural progression, but \nit meets head on with Saudi \nArabia’s enduring passion \nfor in-person shopping. This \ndynamic has given rise to \nomni-channel strategies \nthat blend online and offline \ntouchpoints as brands adapt \nto meet Saudi shoppers \nwherever they are. \nFrom surging e-commerce \nadoption to ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-039", "title": "39The Saudi government’s ", "content": "39The Saudi government’s \npush for a digital economy \nhas set the stage for robust \ne-commerce expansion. \nInternet penetration has \nreached 99% in Saudi Arabia, \nand nearly all adults use \nsmartphones, creating a \nready base for online retail. \nIn fashion, online sales have \nskyrocketed.18 Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion e-commerce market \nwas valued at approximately E-Commerce Is\nRising, But not at the \nExpense of Stores05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nand is projected to exceed USD \n7 .6 billion by 2029.19 Half of \nrespondents reported buying \nmost of their fashion online, \nwhile 35% said t", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-040", "title": "40Despite the rise of ", "content": "40Despite the rise of \ne-commerce, Saudi consumers \nremain deeply connected \nto mall culture. In a country \nwhere malls serve as social \nhubs and leisure destinations, \nshopping is more than a \ntransaction, it is a lifestyle and \nform of entertainment. This \ncultural preference ensures \nthat physical retail not only \nendures, but continues \nto thrive alongside online \nshopping.05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nThe strength of Saudi mall \nculture has made the retail \nsector relatively resilient to \nthe e-commerce onslaught \nseen elsewhere. Saudi mall \noperators note that local \ncustomers", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-041", "title": "41Why Omni-Channel, ", "content": "41Why Omni-Channel, \nWhy Now?05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nSaudi shoppers comfortably \nuse multiple channels. They \nexpect a seamless journey \nwhether they are scrolling \nthrough a mobile app at \nhome or browsing in-store \nat their favorite shopping \ndestination. Ubiquitous digital \nadoption is a major factor in \nthis development. In 2024, \n79% of all retail transactions \nacross sectors involved \ncashless electronic payments, \nindicating comfort with digital \ncommerce infrastructure.5 In \naddition, consumer behavior \noften blends channels: a \ncustomer might discover a \nfashion item ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-042", "title": "4205. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT", "content": "4205. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\n42Across the Kingdom, there \nis a common pattern of \nconsumers carrying out \nonline research before \nbuying in a physical store, \nand retailers have responded \nby ensuring their online \nchannels complement the \nstore experience. For example, \nmany retailers enable real-\ntime inventory checks in \nnearby stores or offer click-\nand-collect services and easy \nin-store returns for online \npurchases. Over the past year, \nseveral leading fashion chains \nin Saudi Arabia have rolled out \nclick-and-collect at dozens of \nbranches, recognizing that \nmany shoppers", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-043", "title": "43", "content": "43\nOn Saudi Arabia’s omni-\nchannel landscape, social \nmedia plays an outsized role \nin fashion engagement. The \nKingdom boasts one of the \nworld’s most active social \nmedia populations, particularly \nwhen it comes to Gen Z, with \nplatforms like Instagram, \nSnapchat, TikT ok, and X acting \nas virtual runways and market \nforums that heavily influence \npurchase decisions. T oday, \nyoung consumers scroll \nthrough influencer posts, \nbrand lookbooks, and style \nvideos to discover the latest \ntrends. A strong majority \n(81%) of survey respondents \nsay it is important that brands \nreflect the latest f", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-044", "title": "44FIGURE 20", "content": "44FIGURE 20\nT op Discovery Channels for New Beauty Products Among Saudi Consumers (%)3TikT ok is the top discovery \nplatform for new beauty \nproducts at 51%, followed \nby peer recommendations \n(43%) and Instagram (40%), \nconfirming the powerful \nrole of short-form video \nand social trust in shaping \nbeauty awareness and \npurchase intent.\nInfluencers as Key \nIntermediaries:\nWith social media acting as \na powerful platform, Saudi \nArabia’s fashion influencer \neconomy has grown into a \ndynamic industry. Hundreds \nof influencers now serve \nas trendsetters and brand \nambassadors with sway \nover Gen", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-045", "title": "45FIGURE 20", "content": "45FIGURE 20\nT op Discovery Channels for New Beauty Products Among Saudi Consumers (%)3\nPersonalization\nacross channels: \nOmni-channel engagement \nin Saudi Arabia is increasingly \npowered by personalization, \nespecially for younger and \naffluent consumers. Retailers \nuse loyalty data and insights \ninto online behavior to \ncustomize digital and in-store \nexperiences. Mobile apps \nhighlight relevant styles, like \nfashion tailored for abaya Blending Online and \nOffline: Omni-Channel \nStrategies in Action05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\nbuyers or streetwear for Gen \nZ men, while in store, l", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-046", "title": "46", "content": "46\n further trend that straddles \nthe online and offline worlds \nis concept stores that blend \ncurated, lifestyle-driven \nshopping with community \nand social media appeal. In \nSaudi Arabia, concept stores \noften feature local designers, \nthemed decor, cafés, and art \ncorners, creating immersive \ncustomer experiences. Their \nhybrid nature, which is part \nboutique, part cultural hub, Concept Stores\nand Immersive\nExperiences05. OMNI-CHANNEL AND DIGIT AL ENGAGEMENT\ncombines physical retail \nwith digital engagement and \ncommunity-building. \nThe scene is rapidly expanding, \nparticularly in Riyadh an", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-047", "title": "47", "content": "47\nROBUST RET AIL LANDSCAPE\nAND INFRASTRUCTURE:\nTHE KEY ROLE OF MALLS  06.\nThe previous edition of The \nState of Fashion Sector in \nSaudi Arabia report (2024) \nhighlighted how Saudi Arabia’s \nfashion market was deeply \nshaped by its mall-driven retail \ninfrastructure. Last year, over \nhalf of the nation’s fashion \nspending was concentrated \nin the three cities of Riyadh, \nJeddah, and Dammam, with \nmalls serving not only as \ncommercial hubs but also \nas key lifestyle and social \ndestinations. As stated in the \n2024 report, the top five mall \noperators (Cenomi, Al Othaim, \nHamat, Alandalus, and ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-048", "title": "48Public and private ", "content": "48Public and private \ndevelopers in Saudi Arabia \nare aggressively building \nnew malls to boost capacity \nand quality, with organized \nretail gross leasable area \n(GLA) in major cities on track \nto double by 2030, fueled by \ngiga-projects and modern \nshopping centers. New \ndevelopments aim to surpass \nexisting regional benchmarks, \nfeaturing cutting-edge \narchitecture, better traffic flow \nand parking access, and an \nelevated tenant mix. As a result \nof these efforts, financiers \nand investors are gaining \nconfidence; sentiment around \nfunding large shopping centers \nhas improved, as strong \nd", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-049", "title": "49", "content": "49\nMalls must cater to a broad \nspectrum of visitors from \nvalue-focused families to \nultra-high-net-worth shoppers. \nAs such, there is a need to \nbalance mass-market and \nluxury retail under one roof. \nIn practice, this means that \nlarge ‘power malls’ anchor \nfast fashion chains, mid-range \nbrands, and department \nstores in high-traffic areas, \nwhile carving out exclusive \nwings or sections for luxury \nboutiques. Mass-market \nretail dominates in terms \nof floor space, footfall, and \nsales volume, with top mall \noperators like Cenomi and \nAl Othaim emphasizing \nglobal fast fashion brands \nand ", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Digital_Fashion_Repo-050", "title": "50A deeper dive into mall ", "content": "50A deeper dive into mall \ndynamics uncovers interesting \npatterns in terms of product \ncategory share and retail \nformats: \nFragrance and Beauty:\nBeauty and personal \ncare accounts for a \ndisproportionate share of \nSaudi retail, with the beauty/\nfragrance category often \nrivaling apparel in count of \nstores. This reflects consumer \nspending patterns; Saudis \noften spend generously on \nperfumes, cosmetics, and \npersonal grooming, making \nthe category a key revenue \ndriver. Culturally, fragrances \nare particularly highly prized. \nMall corridors are filled with \nperfume boutiques, including \na m", "source": "market/Digital_Fashion_Report_2025.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-001", "title": "The Saudi guide", "content": "The Saudi guide\nto marketing and\ncomplianceDirect Marketing: Overview\nhouranipartners.com\nFeatured article on:", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-002", "title": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overvi", "content": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overview ...\nCopyright © Thomson Reuters Canada Limited or its licensors. All rights reserved. 1Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia)\nby Jennifer Abou Fadel  and Ellen Ray, Hourani & Partners\nRelated Content\nPractice note: overview | Law stated as at 01-Oct-2024 | Middle East, Saudi Arabia\nA Practice Note providing an overview of the legal issues surrounding direct marketing in the\nKingdom of Saudi Arabia. This Note considers the statutes, regulations, and voluntary codes of\npractice that apply to direct marketing ac", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-003", "title": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overvi", "content": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overview...\nCopyright © Thomson Reuters Canada Limited or its licensors. All rights reserved. 2 \nThere are stringent regulatory rules on the collection, use, and sharing of consumer Personal\nData for marketing purposes. Businesses must obtain explicit consent from consumers before\nusing their Personal Data for marketing purposes, ensuring transparency and protecting\nprivacy.\n \nThere are also regulatory safeguards against deceptive, misleading, or unfair practices in\ndirect marketing. Marketing content must comply with co", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-004", "title": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overvi", "content": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overview...\nCopyright © Thomson Reuters Canada Limited or its licensors. All rights reserved. 3\"any data, regardless of its source or form, that may lead to identifying an individual\nspecifically, or that may directly or indirectly make it possible to identify an individual,\nincluding name, personal identification number, addresses, contact numbers, license\nnumbers, records, personal assets, bank and credit card numbers, photos and videos\nof an individual, and any other data of personal nature.\"\n \nThe PDPL mainly deals w", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-005", "title": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overvi", "content": "Direct Marketing: Overview (Saudi Arabia), Practical Law UK Practice Note Overview...\nCopyright © Thomson Reuters Canada Limited or its licensors. All rights reserved. 4 \n•The Data Subject must give their explicit consent. Consent is considered explicit if:\n•it is given by the Data Subject in a form that clearly indicates that the Data Subject\napproves the processing of their Personal Data;\n•it is given in a manner that cannot be interpreted otherwise; and\n•given in a manner that can be proven (that is, in writing).\n(Article 1.9, IR.)\n•The Data Subject must consent voluntarily and with a full ", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Direct_Marketing_Ove-006", "title": "Read our full overview", "content": "Read our full overview\nof Saudi Arabia’s\ndirect marketing regulations\non Practical Law ME website\nor contact the authors directly.", "source": "market/Direct-Marketing-Overview-Saudi-Arabia-Web-PDF.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-001", "title": "Effat University Repository", "content": "Effat University Repository\nHow Do Influencers Affect the\nBuying Behavior of Females in KSA\nItem TypeCapstone\nAuthors Harb, Tala\nPublisher Effat University\nDownload date 2026-06-06 11:47:28\nLink to Itemhttp://hdl.handle.net/20.500.14131/1711\n\nHow Do Influencers Affect the Buying Behavior of Females in KSA\nBy \nTala Harb\nA Dissertation Submitted in Partial Fulfillment of the Requirements of the Degree \nBachelor’s in Marketing\nCollege of business \nEffat University\nThesis Advisor: Dr Zainah Qasem\n2024\n\nEffat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n2\n\nEffat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n3", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-002", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nAbstract\nThe purpose of this study is to explore social media influencers influence on women in Saudi \nArabia. Increasingly more companies employ the capacities of influencer marketing. It allows \nbrands to reach their audience in a new way especially due to the fact that modern Saudi women \nare fairly active on such social platforms as, for example, Instagram, Snapchat, Twitter, and \nothers. Western researchers have conducted numerous studies on influencer marketings well as \nuncovered its logics and peculiarities however, the subtleties and effectiveness of", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-003", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nAcknowledgements\nI am deeply grateful to the people who have guided me for the duration of the adventure of \npenning this research. First and fundamental, I amplify my heartfelt appreciation to my manager \nDr Zainah Qasem, for her useful steering and unwavering guide. Her encouragement and \nunderstanding have been crucial in determining the path of this artwork. In addition, I extend my \ngratitude to Effat University for providing the centers and vital resources needed to complete \nthese studies. Not to mention, I owe a huge debt of gratitude to my own family", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-004", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nTable of Contents \n1.Introduction............................................................................................................................... 8\n2.Literature review:.................................................................................................................... 10\n2.1 role of social media influencers ............................................................................................... 10\n2.2 cultural context and influencer impact ...................................................................................", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-005", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nHow do influencers affect the buying behavior of females in KSA\n1.Introduction\nInfluencers on social media have therefore grown to be an effective marketing tool. Depending \non the content and message structure, certain influencers in Saudi Arabia are the most useful for \nfostering social selling and boosting online sales. As a result, the purpose of this study was to \nassess how social media influencers affect consumers’ opinions on what to buy. This study \nresearch question is How do influencers affect the buying behavior of females in KSA, and it is \nfocus", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-006", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nUnderstanding the impact of influencers is of big importance to track how it affects the buying \nbehavior of females in KSA.This research paper will get into the details of influencer marketing \nin the Saudi market exploring how the social intermediate powers work, the mechanism by  \nwhich it influence consumer perception, and finally, the extent to which it influences their \npurchase.This paper will utilize a holistic strategy, combining the primary data, secondary data, \nthe quantitative research instrument, and sample size to achieve this goal. \nBy conduct", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-007", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n2.Literature Review: \n2.1 Role of Social Media Influencers\nSocial media influencers (SMIs)  have a big impact on Saudi Arabian women\'s purchasing \ndecisions (Alalwan et al., 2020). According to Al-Debei et al. (2019), influencers use popular \nplatforms like Instagram, Snapchat, Tiktok,  and Twitter to present lifestyles that appeal to their \nfollowers, especially young girls. Influencers are portrayed in this way as trend-setters and role \nmodels who affect the tastes and purchasing decisions of their followers.\n \nThrough positive advertising, SMIs prompt the", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-008", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nshopping malls and centers, to keep pace with the Kingdom’s goals of Vision 2030 ( Algumzi et \nal., 2022). This has led people to go out more often, which in result leads to people purchasing \nmore often. Previously, people in Saudi Arabia typically  cooked and ate at home or at a friend’s \nhouse, and dining out in a restaurant was somewhat frowned upon. But during the last decade, \nfoodservice restaurants grew more popular ( Leung et al., 2022).\n \n2.3 Authenticity and Relatability\nInfluencers\' capability to connect with their audience and  to be honest with ", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-009", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n2.5 Factors Influencing Influencer Impact\nInfluencer credibility, product relevance, and amount  of follower engagement are some of the \nmain variables that affect how influencers  affect consumers\' purchasing decisions (Alshurideh et \nal., 2022). These factors determine whether influencer marketing efforts work in Saudi Arabia\'s \nmarket. \n \nSocial media influencers (SMIs) have a great effect on the consumer’s buying behavior in Saudi \nArabia. The influence has especially increased during the Covid-19 pandemic due to lockdown, \nwhich has caused many people to", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-010", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n3.Methodology: \nThis part of the research paper discusses the methodology which is the tools used in order to \ndevelop our research and how we collected information in order to complete the research. It \nincludes the primary data, secondary data, quantitative research tool, and sample size. Each part \nis explained in detail on how we managed to gather and collect the information we needed.\n3.1 Secondary Data\nThis research paper includes both primary and secondary data. Secondary data is information \nbased on already existing data. Secondary data has already b", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-011", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nended questions that cover all important topics and problems of our research to help us gain more\nknowledge and all the information we need regarding how influencers have a huge impact in  \ntoday’s world. The survey will be distributed among Saudi citizens, their response will be \nanalyzed and evaluated as well.\n3.3 Quantitative Research\nThis research is based on quantitative data. Quantitative methods are responsible for data that \ncan be counted or measured in numerical values. It is a type of study methodology that centers \non gathering and evaluating meas", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-012", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\ndifferent perspectives. This will help us understand the topic better. To make sure our group of \nindividuals represents everyone well, we will use a method called convenience sampling. It\'s \nessential to note that, while we had alternative sampling methods at our disposal, we deliberately\nchose convenience sampling for specific reasons. This method permits us to select participants \nbased on their accessibility and availability. In essence, it empowers us to gather dependable and \ncredible data concerning our topic as it is cheap, efficient, and simple to im", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-013", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\n4.Results:\nThis section presents descriptive statistics which include several questions from the survey in \norder to understand and gather information from female consumers in relation to influencers. \nThe number of responses was 50 coming only from females in order to carry out this research in \nthe right way. \n4.1 Descriptive Statistics \nFigure 1. Responders age \nFigure 1 shows 92% of the respondents’ ages ranged from 18-24, since this is the average age of \nconsumers are influenced more by purchasing or following influencers on different platforms. \nThat a", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-014", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 2. Responders status \nFigure 2 provides the state of marital status confirms valuable insights into the demographic \ninformation that can be used in market segmentation. The higher percentage of females were \nsingle with an 82%, and 18% of the respondents are married which indicates  a notable \ndemographic trend along the sample population.\nFigure 3. Responders Residence \nFigure 3 shows the region is KSA and it is related to track down which city holds the most \ninteraction regarding the geographical locations and  diversity. It focused on two highest-", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-015", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 4. Most\nused platform \nFigure 4 allows us to understand as marketers carrying this research, which platform is used by \nthe consumers and where does the influencing on buying behaviors lie.   In the graph, instagram \nhas the highest interaction with 74.5%, next comes snapchat with 60.8% and close to it is Tiktok.\nThose platforms are indeed the most used by females for entertaining and buying purposes. \nThat’s why influencers focus mainly on those 3 platforms to carry out their advertisements or \nhow they show products and goods to the viewer.  \nFigure ", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-016", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 5 is shows a  yes” or “no” question to generally know if females follow influencers and \nbrowse or they don’t .  90% of the answers were a yes, showing the widespread influence of \nsocial media relation within this demographic.  Conversely, only 1 0% of the respondents  were a \nno indicating they don’t follow influencers, which suggests a minority within the population who\ndon’t engage with influencer content.  \nFigure 6. Number of followed influencers \nFigure 6 was related to the previous one in regard to the individuals who put “yes” as an option \nto", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-017", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 7. Preferred products percentage result  \nFigure 7 shows how i t’s important to know what consumers search for the most when they \nfollow influencers, or what interests them and affects their buying behavior. Every person has \ntheir own likings and preferences when it comes to purchasing a product, that’s why knowing \nwhich product or service are promoted mostly by influencers and liked by the individuals . \nBecause this questionnaire targeted females only, the higher percentage was 52%, for fashion \nand beauty. Since they care so much about their phys", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-018", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 8. Purchasing Frequency percentage result \nFigure 8 represents the  frequency of the individuals purchasing different products or services that\ninfluencers recommend is vital to our research since it shows how impactful or not, they are to \nthe buying behavior. The options ranged from “very frequently” to “never” as to measure how \nfrequently they buy.  The results show that many people follow influencers, but not all purchase \nfrom what they offer or show on screen. 31.4% of the respondents rarely purchase from \ninfluencers and 29.4% occasionally purc", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-019", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 9. Decision influence percentage result \nFigure 9 shows the different factors that somehow push the individual to purchase the product or \nservice that influencer recommends range from having trust in the influencer, their relatability, \nthe discounts they offer, or none of the factors could influence the behavior.  The highest \npercentage factor that affects buying the displayed product by the influencers is “product \nrelevance to my interest” with 54.9% , meaning that females care so much on how this product \nor service comes along with what they lik", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-020", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 10 aims to assess the extent of which influencers can influence their followers solely by \nrecommending the product. 66.7% of the respondents chose “Yes” while the rest 33.3% chose \n“No”. This indicates that the majority of the respondents can get influenced to purchase a \nproduct if the influencer they follow recommends it. This also suggests that the level of influence\ninfluencers have is considered high.  \nFigure 11. Rating credibility of influencers \nFigure 11 shows that 33.3% of the participants chose “Somewhat credible”, 31.4% chose “Very \ncredib", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-021", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 12. Trusting influencers or not percentage results \nFigure 12 shows 49% of the participants chose “Not sure” and 13.7% chose “No” which makes \nup most of the respondents. This shows that the majority are not sure whether their influencers \nactually use the products they advertise . 37.3% chose “Yes\'\' which is a relatively significant \nportion of the sample who believe that influencers in fact use the products they promote.\nFigure 13. Assessing Authenticity\nFigure 13 presents 41.2% of respondents say “Transparency about sponsorship or partnerships” \nwhi", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-022", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 14. Reflection of accuracy of influencer percentage results\nFigure 14 shows that there are different perspectives among the female’s opinions. 62.5% of the \nresponses answered affirmatively, and 12.5% disagreed, and a notable 12.5% are uncertain \nwhich highlights the diversity of opinions on this matter. \nFigure 15.\nDisappointment percentage level \nFigure 15 reported that many respondents, 74.1%, being disappointed with a product or service \npurchased. This suggests that consumers are less satisfied with the effectiveness of products  \napproved by infl", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-023", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nFigure 16. Evolvement of Influencer industry \nFigure 16 shows the majority (55.4%) say growth in influencer diversity and specialization . A \n25% foresee increased regulation and transparency . A small percentage (8.9%) predict a decline \nin the influencer’s influence. These trends suggest dynamic changes ahead for the industry.\nFigure 17. Relation of influencer marketing and traditional advertising \nFigure 17 shows that  78.6% of participants think that influencer marketing has become more \nimportant. While 16.1% believe that traditional advertising techniqu", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-024", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nArabia.\nA complement of SMIs is their influence on female consumers of Saudi Arabia, where SMIs \nconsiderably influence their decisions to buy or associate with. Influencers’ way of life and \nendorsements influence their followers to purchase something, try new habits, and influence their\nfollowers through the way of living. A comprehensive questionnaire was developed during this \nresearch; it entailed 21 questions from different scales, with Likert and open-ended questions, \nand 50 female respondents that aimed to collect information on influencer marketing ", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-025", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nPreferred Social Media Platforms  \nInstagram has become the platform, for female participants closely followed by Snapchat and \nTikTok. This emphasizes the importance for marketers to concentrate their influencer marketing \nefforts on these platforms to connect effectively with their target audience.\nFollowing Influencers\nA large majority (90%) of participants mentioned that they follow influencers on media \nindicating the significant role influencers play in influencing consumer preferences and buying \ndecisions.\nInfluencer Impact on Purchasing Behavior\nThe ", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-026", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nthe country\'s increased number of cities, infrastructure, and events, which have contributed to the\ncountry\'s cultural landscape by exposing women to greater freedom, accessibility, and inspiration\nto pursue further knowledge and investigation into the lifestyles they envision for \nthemselves.Influencers boost audience engagement and trust by incorporating cultural values, \ncustoms, and promotions of all types in beauty, travel, music, local tourism, and fitness into their\ncontent.\nAuthenticity and Relatability\n \nBuilding trust with their audience through sin", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Dissertation_Researc-027", "title": "Effat University", "content": "Effat University\nMGT490SPRING 2024\nbuy. Furthermore, the results indicate that these consumers place a higher value on celebrity \nendorsements than on product quality and price. Furthermore, the majority of respondents \nthought that the corporations primarily leveraged celebrity endorsements to boost sales and \nprofits. Therefore, it can be said that a celebrity’s attractiveness, how they utilize a product, their \ngood public perception, and the fact that they are perceived as celebrities all have a significant \nimpact on consumers’ decisions to buy products.\nOur findings highlight the strong ", "source": "market/Dissertation Research- Tala Harb.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
