    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-001", "title": "We praise God for the remarkable progress our ", "content": "We praise God for the remarkable progress our \nnation has achieved in less than a decade – progress \nthat has positioned Saudi Arabia as a global \nbenchmark for transformation. We take great pride \nin the unwavering dedication of our citizens, whose \nefforts have laid the foundations for a new era of \ngrowth. Together, we carry the journey of progress \nforward, united in shaping the future of our country \nfor generations to come.\nCustodian of the Two Holy Mosques\nKing Salman bin Abdulaziz Al Saud", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-002", "title": "Nine years into Vision 2030, we stand proud of what ", "content": "Nine years into Vision 2030, we stand proud of what \nour people have accomplished. They have turned \nambition into action and goals into milestones. We \nhave not only met key targets – we have surpassed \nmany. Looking ahead, our resolve is stronger than \never. We will accelerate delivery, embrace every \nopportunity, and further elevate the Kingdom’s \nposition as a global leader.\nCrown Prince and Prime Minister His Royal Highness\nPrince  Mohammed bin Salman Al Saud", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-003", "title": "T able of Contents", "content": "T able of Contents\n01 \nExecutive Summary of Vision 2030 Performance \n ◆Overview of the Economic Landscape\n ◆Performance of VRPs and National Strategies\n ◆Performance Across the PillarsIntroduction\nVision Realization Programs  \nNational Strategies\n03\nAn Empowered Society\n ◆Nurturing Talent: Enabling Saudi Human Capital\n ◆Easing Access to Health Services Through Innovation\n ◆Expanding Access to Housing\n ◆A Responsible Society: Non-Profit Sector and Volunteerism 02\nA Fast-Growing Economy\n ◆Economic Diversification and Global Standing\n ◆Public Investment Fund: A Catalyst for Economic Growth\n ◆Empo", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-004", "title": "◆Enhancing Security Across the Kingdom", "content": "◆Enhancing Security Across the Kingdom\n ◆Promoting Health and Inclusion Through Sport\n04\nA Leading Destination  \n ◆Delivering a Seamless Pilgrim Experience\n ◆Tourism Driving Growth and Opportunity\n ◆Cultural Renaissance\n ◆Expanding Entertainment Experiences\n05\nA Sustainable Vision\n ◆Food and Water Security: Building Sustainable Foundations \n ◆Strengthening Water Supplies  \n ◆A Greener Future: Combating Diversification and Restoring \nEcosystems \n ◆Biodiversity: A Protected Natural Heritage \n ◆Powering A Cleaner Future\n06\nA Year of Global Engagement and Local \nMomentum\nAppendix\n5191\n193\n201\n2201", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-005", "title": "As Vision 2030 marks its ninth ", "content": "As Vision 2030 marks its ninth \nyear, Saudi Arabia  stands at a \npivotal moment. The Kingdom \nis delivering on its promise of \ntransformation – empowering its \npeople, reshaping its economic \nlandscape, and unlocking new \nopportunities at scale. The past \nyear has delivered concrete \nprogress across economic, social, \nand institutional fronts confirming \nthe strength of the Kingdom’s \nstrategic direction and the \ncommitment driving it.\nVision 2030 is built on the \nKingdom’s core strengths: its \ncentral role in the Arab and Islamic \nworlds, its influence within the \nglobal economy, and its stra", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-006", "title": "Over the past year, several Vision ", "content": "Over the past year, several Vision \n2030 targets have been reached \nahead of schedule. Others have \nbeen exceeded entirely. But the \nprogress isn’t only in numbers – \nit’s seen in improved quality of life, \ngreater opportunity, and renewed \nconfidence in what’s possible.\nThis report outlines the milestones \nachieved in 2024 and the \ncontinued steps being taken to \ndeliver on Vision 2030. It reflects \na collective national effort – \ndriven by leadership, enabled by \ninstitutions, and brought to life by \nthe people of Saudi Arabia. It’s \nthrough this shared commitment \nthat the Kingdom continues", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-007", "title": "88 Vision  Realization", "content": "88 Vision  Realization\n Programs\nThe Vision Realization Programs (VRPs) have played a central role \nin delivering Vision 2030’s objectives – strengthening coordination, \nbuilding capabilities across the ecosystem, breaking silos, and \naccelerating progress.\nLaunched in the initial phase of Vision 2030, the VRPs were designed \nas the primary implementation vehicles for turning the Vision into \naction. As medium-term programs, the VRPs have evolved over \ntime to remain aligned with national priorities, adapting to changing \neconomic and developmental needs across each phase of Vision \n2030.Some ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-008", "title": "9 National", "content": "9 National\nStrategies\nSaudi Arabia’s national strategies – whether sectoral or regional \n– emerged as a core part of Vision 2030’s delivery model. \nThese strategies drive value creation across key sectors and \nare designed to extend their impact well beyond 2030.\nThese strategies build on the foundation laid by VRPs. As \nprogress accelerated and opportunities became more defined, \nthe need for specialized national and regional strategies \nemerged, ensuring more targeted planning and implementation. \nThis is enhancing economic diversification, expanding impact \nacross regions, and delivering ta", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-009", "title": "10انتقل للملحقات لمعرفة المزيد01 02 03", "content": "10انتقل للملحقات لمعرفة المزيد01 02 03\n04 05 06\n07 08 09\n10 11 12\n13 Integrated Strategy for\n Mining and Mineral\nIndustries Comprehensive\n Strategy for the Real\nEstate Sector Labor Market\nStrategy\nAsir Region\n Development Strategy\n Custodian of the\n Two Holy Mosques\n Scholarship\nProgramFinancial Technology \nStrategyNational  Gaming and \nEsports Strategy\nNational \nBiotechnology \nStrategy\nTransformation Strategy for the Institute \nof Public Administration National  Transport and\nLogistics  Strategy\nNational Industry  \nStrategy National Investment\nStrategy\nNational Red Sea \nSustainability  \nStrat", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-010", "title": "Executive Summary", "content": "Executive Summary\nof Vision 2030 Performance 01\n11Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-011", "title": "This coordinated approach has ", "content": "This coordinated approach has \naccelerated delivery across all \nthree Vision 2030 pillars. By the \nclose of 2024, many performance \nindicators had already \nsurpassed their 2030 targets \n– particularly in areas linked to \neconomic diversification, social \ndevelopment, and institutional \nefficiency. These outcomes have \nelevated national ambition and \nreinforced confidence in the \nVision’s trajectory.\nWhile some indicators have \nfaced technical or economic \nheadwinds, corrective actions are \nunderway and closely monitored. \nVision 2030’s performance \nframework continues to evolve \nthrough enhanc", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-012", "title": "13global best practices. In some ", "content": "13global best practices. In some \ncases, indicators have been \nreplaced with more precise \nalternatives to better reflect \nprogress across transformation \npriorities, reinforcing the Vision’s \nadaptability to regional and global \ndevelopments.\nThis second phase of Vision 2030 \nhas also seen the maturation \nof national planning processes, \nfinancial governance, and \nperformance management \nsystems. These advancements \nreflect nine years of accumulated \nexperience, strengthened \nby partnerships with global \norganizations, and the steady \ndevelopment of technical expertise \nacross sectors. Effort", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-013", "title": "Overview of ", "content": "Overview of \nthe Economic Landscape\nIn 2024, Saudi Arabia maintained \nmacroeconomic stability while \nadvancing structural reforms under \nVision 2030. The economy continued \nto diversify, supported by targeted \ninvestment, policy shifts, and the gradual expansion of non-oil \nsectors. These developments \ncontributed to steady growth and \na favorable investment climate, \nreinforcing the broader trajectory of \nlong-term transformation.\n141.3%Non-Oil Growth Driving Economic Expansion\n2024 marked a year of strong momentum in non-oil sectors. The \ncontinued expansion of investment and private sector ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-014", "title": "15 58.1", "content": "15 58.1\n pointsConfidence in the Private Sector\nPrivate sector performance in 2024 remained \nstrong, with robust growth in new business \nactivity and positive market sentiment.Saudi Purchasing \nManagers’ Index (PMI)7%Labor Market Progress and Unemployment Decline\nThe transformation of the labor market continues to \nyield measurable results. This decline in the Saudi \nunemployment rate reflects the creation of quality \njob opportunities across diversified sectors and \nimproved workforce participation.\nDown from 12.3% in 2016\nAchieving the Vision 2030 target of 7%\nInflation Control and Economic ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-015", "title": "Leading credit rating agencies reaffirmed their ", "content": "Leading credit rating agencies reaffirmed their \nconfidence in Saudi Arabia’s fiscal outlook and long-\nterm economic prospects.\nAA3, stable outlook \nMoody’s\nA+, stable outlook\nFitch\nA/A-1, stable outlook\nS&PCredit Strength\n16Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement\n\nPositive Growth Forecasts from \nGlobal Institutions\n17Saudi Ministry of Finance\n4.6%\n2025 20263.5%OECD\n3.8%\n2025 20263.6%World Bank\n3.4%\n2025 20265.4%IMF\n3.0%\n2025 20263.7%Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Lea", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-016", "title": "18Vision 2030 Progress ", "content": "18Vision 2030 Progress \nOverview\ninternal variables affecting certain \nprograms and strategies. Corrective \nactions are being implemented \nto accelerate delivery and realign \ninitiatives and indicators, ensuring \nthat Vision 2030 objectives remain \nwithin reach.Performance of VRPs and \nNational Strategies\nAs delivery vehicles, the performance \nof VRPs and national strategies is \nevaluated along two main dimensions: \nprogress in initiative execution \nand achievement of performance \nindicators. Overall, efforts remain on \ntrack, with some deviations observed \ndue to fast-evolving external and Ex", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-017", "title": "19¹ In 2024, the number of initiatives increased by 438, following the inclusion", "content": "19¹ In 2024, the number of initiatives increased by 438, following the inclusion of national \nstrategy initiatives that entered the implementation phase.\n299\n Indicators fully achieved,\n including 257 that\nsurpassed their targets49\nIndicators achieved \nbetween 85%-99%  \nof their target374\n Indicators currently have\nactive readingsKPIs\n93%VRP and national strategy \nKPIs either fully achieved \nor on track for 2024 \n674\nInitiatives completed since \nthe launch of Vision 20301596\n Initiatives are\ncurrently on track1,502\nActive initiatives\nin totalInitiatives\nInitiatives either \ncompleted or progres", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-018", "title": "20These shifts are expanding ", "content": "20These shifts are expanding \ninvestment flows, generating private \nsector opportunities, and improving \nthe resilience of the national \neconomy.\nAs part of the Ambitious Nation  \npillar, the Kingdom is accelerating \ndigital transformation, improving \nthe efficiency of public services, \nand deepening environmental \nsustainability efforts. Saudi Arabia’s \nglobal standing has also advanced, \nreflected in stronger international \nrankings and recognition.\nVolunteerism and civic engagement \ncontinue to rise, reinforcing a shared \nsense of purpose.Performance Across \nthe Pillars\nVision 2030 continue", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-019", "title": "21", "content": "21\nA Vibrant Society\nIncreased the number of Saudi sites registered on UNESCO\'s World \nHeritage List, achieving the 2030 target six years ahead of schedule\nAchieved 2030 Target\n8\nSites8\nSitesRecorded a historic number of foreign Umrah pilgrims, exceeding the \n2024 target\nAchieved 2024 Target\n16.92 M\nPilgrims\nIncreased Saudi home ownership, surpassing the 2024 target\nAchieved 2024 Target\n65.4% 64%11.3M\nPilgrimsExecutive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-020", "title": "A Thriving EconomyIncrease in the Public Investment Fund\'s assets under manageme", "content": "A Thriving EconomyIncrease in the Public Investment Fund\'s assets under management, \nsurpassing the 2024 target\nAchieved 2024 Target\n$941.33B $880BIncrease in unlocked investment opportunities, surpassing \nthe 2024 target\nAchieved 2024 Target\n1,865 1,197\n22\nExecutive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement\n\n23\nAn Ambitious Nation The Kingdom\'s ranking in the United Nations E-Government\nDevelopment Index (UN EGDI) jumped 25 places\nAchieved 2024 Target\n6th\nRank\nThe number of volunteers increased, exceeding the 20", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-021", "title": "24Progress Overview   ", "content": "24Progress Overview   \nLevel 1 and Level 2 Vision 2030 KPIs\nTarget Surpassed\nPartially Achieved\n(%85–%99)Target Achieved\nBelow TargetThese KPIs are measured \nperiodically to monitor progress on \nthe Kingdom’s social and economic \ntransformation priorities.\nCorrective actions are taken for \nany off-track indicators through \ncoordinated implementation \nplans across sectors. At the \nsame time, continuous efforts are \nmade to improve measurement \nmethodologies and align them with \ninternational best practices.The Vision 2030 KPIs at Levels 1 and \n2 measure the Kingdom’s progress \ntoward the strate", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-022", "title": "A Vibrant ", "content": "A Vibrant \nSociety\n25Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-023", "title": "Following Vision 2030’s launch, ", "content": "Following Vision 2030’s launch, \nthe methodology for calculating \nthis KPI was updated, setting the \n2016 baseline at 6.2 million foreign \npilgrims.\nSince 2022, the number of foreign \nUmrah pilgrims has consistently \nexceeded annual targets for three \nconsecutive years, including a record \n16.92 million in 2024, reflecting \na steady recovery and growth \ntrajectory after the temporary decline \ncaused by the COVID-19 pandemic.\nThis growth is driven by a wide range of initiatives designed to \nmake pilgrimage more accessible, \nincluding:\n• Simplifying entry procedures at \nports and airports\n• Expa", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-024", "title": "The original Vision 2030 target to ", "content": "The original Vision 2030 target to \ndouble the number of Saudi sites on \nthe UNESCO World Heritage List has \nbeen achieved. In 2023, \"The Cultural \nLandscape of Al-Faw Archaeological \nArea\" became the Kingdom’s eighth \nlisted site, meeting the 2030 target \nahead of schedule.This progress is the result of \nintegrated efforts between cultural, \ntourism, and urban development \nbodies focused on site rehabilitation, \ninternational nominations, and global \npromotion of Saudi heritage. These \nactions continue to strengthen Saudi \nArabia’s global cultural positioning \nand highlight the depth of its h", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-025", "title": "The initial target was to raise the rate to ", "content": "The initial target was to raise the rate to \n52% by 2020, but that was surpassed \nearly, reaching 60%. As a result, the \ntarget for 2030 was raised to 70% .\nIn 2024, the rate reached 65.4%, \nexceeding the year’s target of 64% . \nThis progress is the result of \ngovernment programs, financial \nsupport, and a wider variety of housing \noptions made available to citizens. Reforms in the housing sector have \nalso made it easier and quicker for \nfamilies to get the support they need \nto buy a home.\nLooking ahead, the outlook remains \nstrong. At the same time, changes in \nthe housing market – such as ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-026", "title": "29This indicator has been tracked since ", "content": "29This indicator has been tracked since \n2017, when less than 1% of financial \naid beneficiaries who were capable \nof working had been empowered.\nSince then, steady progress has \nbeen made, with the empowerment \nrate rising significantly year on year, \nsurpassing both the 2024 and 2025 \ntargets ahead of schedule.\nThe improvement reflects ongoing \nefforts to transition eligible \nbeneficiaries toward greater self-\nreliance by expanding access \nto digital services, increasing \nawareness, and building technical capabilities in areas such as artificial \nintelligence and data analysis.\nBeneficiaries", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-027", "title": "Despite a slight drop in global ", "content": "Despite a slight drop in global \nranking from 28th in 2023 to 32nd in \n2024, Saudi Arabia maintains a score \nof 6.6, above both the global average \n(5.53) and the Gulf average (6.4).\nThis reflects the Kingdom’s \nintegrated efforts across several \nVision Realization Programs – \nparticularly in health, quality of life, \nhousing, and human capability \ndevelopment.Progress has been supported by \nincreased access to healthcare, \nrising life expectancy, enhanced \nurban livability, and improvements in \ngovernance, justice, and \nanti-corruption efforts. Baseline 2024\nActual Value   2024\nTarget2030\nTar", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-028", "title": "31The indicator’s measurement ", "content": "31The indicator’s measurement \nmethodology was updated by the World \nHealth Organization to ensure greater \naccuracy and alignment with global \nstandards, resulting in an updated \nbaseline of 77.06 years.Life expectancy reached 78.8 years \nin 2023, exceeding targets ahead of \nschedule and placing the Kingdom 11th \namong G20 countries. This progress \nreflects reforms to the healthcare system, \nimproved preventive care, better nutrition \nand public health awareness, and a shift \ntoward community-based services.Baseline  2023\nActual ValueTarget\n2023  2030\nTarget  \n77 .06 78 .2 80\n78.8Life Expecta", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-029", "title": "Between 2019 and 2023, healthcare ", "content": "Between 2019 and 2023, healthcare \ncoverage expanded significantly, \nespecially during and following \nthe COVID-19 response, with the \ncoverage rate rising sharply from \n86% to 96.4% .\n The indicator surpassed its annual \ntargets for 2024 and 2025 and is now \napproaching its 2026 target. It did so through mobile clinic rollouts, local \nhealth service delivery, expanded \ninsurance coverage, and improved \nhealthcare infrastructure, reducing \nthe need for patient transfers.\nBy 2025, five new hospitals across \nfive regions are expected to \nopen, further advancing the 2030 \nhealthcare coverage targ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-030", "title": "The index was recently added to the ", "content": "The index was recently added to the \nlist of level 1 and 2 KPIs. This index \nmeasures service quality across \nfive dimensions: patient focus, \neffectiveness, efficiency, safety, and \nfairness. With support from the World \nHealth Organization (WHO), the index \nmethodology was developed to track \noutcomes as service delivery scales.Progress in 2023 exceeded \nexpectations, with marked \nimprovements in operational \nperformance, reduced wait times, \nand enhanced patient satisfaction.\n \nDriven by ongoing efforts under \nthe Health Sector Transformation \nProgram, along with planned \ninitiatives underw", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-031", "title": "Originally part of a broader Vision ", "content": "Originally part of a broader Vision \n2030 KPI, the measure was later \nsplit into two: one for children and \nadolescents, and another for adults. \nThis change brings the indicator in \nline with international standards and \nimproves comparability with other \ncountries.\nSince the baseline was set in \n2022, the indicator has improved \nsignificantly—rising by over 12% in \njust two years, and exceeding the \n2024 target of 10%.This progress reflects ongoing \nnational efforts to promote sports \nand physical activity through:\n• Expanded access to sports \nfacilities and clubs\n• School and university spo", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-032", "title": "35", "content": "35\nThis is one of Vision 2030’s \noriginal commitments. It was \nrefined and replaced with two \ninternationally aligned indicators \nto ensure compatibility with global \nmeasurement standards and enable \ncross-country comparisons - this \nbeing one of them.\nIn just two years, the indicator \nsurpassed annual targets through \n2027.Progress was driven by the \ndevelopment of safe and accessible \nenvironments for physical activity, \nsustained investment in public sports \ninfrastructure, and the creation \nof parks and dedicated fitness \npathways across cities. Community \nsports events, such as the Riyad", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-033", "title": "This index is published in the \"Global ", "content": "This index is published in the \"Global \nLivability Ranking\" report issued by \nthe Economist Intelligence Unit (EIU), \nand measures five key dimensions: \nstability, culture and environment, \neducation, health, and infrastructure.\n \nWhile no Saudi city has yet entered \nthe top 100, several cities have \nimproved their rankings, despite \na drop in 2022 due to the EIU’s \ninclusion of 33 new cities.The improvement seen in 2024 \nreflects increased investment in \ninfrastructure, education, and \nhealthcare. \nAs regional strategies continue to roll \nout, further progress is expected in \nthe coming years", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-034", "title": "The Environmental Performance Index ", "content": "The Environmental Performance Index \n(EPI), published by Yale University, ranks \ncountries on environmental health and \necosystem vitality. \nThis decline is largely due to changes \nin the EPI’s measurement methodology \nand limited access to Saudi \nenvironmental data by international \nevaluators, despite active national \nprograms addressing air quality, waste \nmanagement, and climate change.To address this, the Kingdom is now \nworking to:\n• Enhance governance around \nenvironmental reporting\n• Strengthen regulatory frameworks \nfor consistent measurement\nThese efforts aim to improve future \nperfo", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-035", "title": "PISA, conducted by the OECD, ", "content": "PISA, conducted by the OECD, \nmeasures student performance in \nmath, science, and reading. In 2022, \nSaudi Arabia’s overall score was 387, \nfalling short of the target.\nWhile performance in science and \nmathematics improved, a decline in \nreading impacted the overall result. Factors affecting this include:\n• Low enrollment in pre-primary \neducation\n• The need to strengthen curricula \nand teacher training\nTo address these challenges, the \nupcoming Education and Training \nStrategy will:\n• Improve curriculum development\n• Strengthen stakeholder \ncoordination\n• Expand extracurricular and skills-\nb", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-036", "title": "39A Thriving ", "content": "39A Thriving \nEconomyExecutive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Global\nEngagement", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-037", "title": "This indicator is part of Vision 2030’s ", "content": "This indicator is part of Vision 2030’s \ncore promises, aiming for Saudi \nArabia to rise from 20th to 15th place \nglobally in terms of GDP.\nIn 2024, the Kingdom adopted a \nchain-linking method to align with \ninternational best practices, leading \nto an update in historical data.Between 2016 and 2024, real GDP \ngrew at a compound annual growth \nrate (CAGR) of 1.75%, with the \nexception of contractions in 2020 \nand 2023.\nAlthough the 2024 target was not \nfully reached, the index achieved \n98% of the goal, driven by a 1.3%  \noverall growth rate, led by a strong \n4.3%  increase in non-oil activiti", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-038", "title": "Also revised under the chain-linking ", "content": "Also revised under the chain-linking \nmethodology, non-oil GDP has \nsteadily increased since 2016, with a \nCAGR of 3.01% . \nDespite falling slightly short of its \n2024 target, the index reached 98% \nof the goal, supported by a 3.9%  \nyear-on-year growth rate.Growth was especially strong in \nwholesale and retail, hospitality, \ntransportation, logistics, and \ntechnology, reflecting the Kingdom’s \nefforts to diversify the economy and \nstrengthen non-oil sectors.Baseline 2024\nActual Value   2024\nTarget 2030\nTarget\n534.27\n6 8 0.9 0694.76 1,325\n41Non-Oil GDP\n)USD Billion (Executive \nSummary A Fast-G", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-039", "title": "This indicator tracks the private ", "content": "This indicator tracks the private \nsector’s share in the economy, a \npromise of Vision 2030.\nIn 2024, the contribution reached \n47%, exceeding the annual target \nand reflecting a CAGR of 1.94% \nsince 2016.\nKey drivers of this growth include:\n• A shift away from oil dependency\n• Expanded investment \nopportunities for the private \nsector\n• PIF-led stimulation of private \nsector involvementSaudi Arabia continues to intensify \nefforts to empower the private \nsector, create a business-friendly \nenvironment, and enhance the role of \nSMEs through regulatory changes, \nmega projects, and national \nstra", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-040", "title": "43", "content": "43\nThis indicator is one of Vision 2030’s \npromises. It measures the share of total \nbank credit facilities directed to small \nand medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) – \na key driver of private sector growth and \neconomic diversification.\nDespite strong performance, the index \nfell slightly short of its annual target. \nThis was primarily due to the overall rise \nin bank credit facilities, driven by higher \ngrowth in other lending portfolios—\nparticularly in real estate financing, \nwhich has supported increased \nhomeownership. Additionally, the \ngrowing availability of alternative lending \nchannels", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-041", "title": "This index is one of Vision 2030’s ", "content": "This index is one of Vision 2030’s \ncommitments, with its latest annual \nmeasurement released for 2023 data.\nLocal content in the oil & gas sector \nreached 65.5%, achieving 99.2% of \nthe annual target. This was driven by supply chain \nlocalization policies, minimum local \ncontent quotas in energy contracts, \nand major initiatives led by Saudi \nAramco and the Ministry of Energy.Baseline 2023\nActual Value  2023\nTarget  2030\nTarget  \n37%\n65.5%66% 75%\n44Local Content Share in the \nOil & Gas Sector \n)Percentage (Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A ", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-042", "title": "With a CAGR of 41%, oil and gas-", "content": "With a CAGR of 41%, oil and gas-\nrelated exports reached 96.3% of the \n2024 target by Q1. Growth has been \nsupported by enhanced financing \ntools for exporters, expansion of chemical exports, and strong global \ndemand for Saudi crude. Future \nperformance will depend on global \neconomic trends and energy market \ndynamics.Baseline  Q1 2024\nActual Value2024\nTarget   2030\nTarget\n$34.36\n$191.62$199.02 $563.59\n45Cumulative Value of Exports from Oil and \nGas-Related Industries\n)USD Billion (Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Yea", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-043", "title": "Despite surpassing targets in earlier ", "content": "Despite surpassing targets in earlier \nyears (2019–2021), performance has \nrecently been affected by:\n• Rising non-oil imports, \ncontributing to higher foreign \neconomic leakage\n• The increasing scale and \ncomplexity of non-oil sector \nprojects, which often require \nimported componentsNonetheless, the government is \nactively working to realign the \nindicator. Current efforts include:\n• Setting sector-specific local \ncontent targets\n• Improving local supply chain \nintegration\n• Advancing initiatives under the \nIndustrial Strategy, Investment \nStrategy, and other localization \nprograms\nWith thes", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-044", "title": "By 2023, the indicator recorded a ", "content": "By 2023, the indicator recorded a \nnotable increase of over % 12 in just \ntwo years, surpassing its annual \ntargets for both 2022 and 2023, \nreflecting accelerated progress.\nThis performance is driven by:\n• The strategic efforts of the \nGeneral Authority for Military \nIndustries (GAMI), established in \n2017\n• The rollout of policies and \nregulations enabling private sector \nparticipation• Programs such as the Industrial \nParticipation Program and the \nSupply Chain Resilience Initiative, \nwhich incentivize local production \nand attract foreign partners to \ninvest in local manufacturing\nThese ef", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-045", "title": "PIF was restructured at the onset ", "content": "PIF was restructured at the onset \nof Vision 2030 to become a global \ninvestment powerhouse and a driver \nof national economic growth.\nAs of 2024, PIF assets reached  \n$0.94 trillion, exceeding the annual \ntarget of $0.88 trillion. \nThis represents a 390 % increase \nsince 2016, with a compound annual \ngrowth rate (CAGR) of 22 %.The strong performance is driven by:\n• A proactive, diversified investment \nstrategy\n• A portfolio with 40%  exposure to \nSaudi companies, including giga-\nprojects\n• Strategic international investments \nin promising global sectors\nDue to this sustained growth, the \norig", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-046", "title": "This World Bank index measures ", "content": "This World Bank index measures \nlogistics efficiency, including \ninfrastructure, customs, and \nshipment timeliness.\nIt is currently under revision as \npart of the National Transport and \nLogistics Strategy (NTLS) to reflect \nupdated ambitions.These gains were driven by major \ninfrastructure upgrades, faster \ncustoms clearance, and expanded \nlogistics services.Baseline 2022\nActual Value  2022\nTarget   2030\nTarget\n49Logistics Performance Index\n)Rank (\n38th49th49th25th \ngloballyExecutive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVision A Year of Glob", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-En_Annual_Report_Vis-047", "title": "This indicator tracks the share of ", "content": "This indicator tracks the share of \nnon-oil exports (goods, services, and \nre-exports) relative to GDP, serving \nas a key measure of economic \ndiversification.\nBetween 2016 and 2024, the \nindicator improved by 7.2% .By the end of 2024, the indicator had \nreached 73%  of its annual target, \ndespite a 13%  year-on-year increase \nin the total value of non-oil exports \nas of 2024.Baseline  2024\nActual Value 2024\nTarget2030\nTarget\n18%\n25.2%35% 50%\n50Non-Oil Exports Share of Non-Oil GDP\n)Percentage (Executive \nSummary A Fast-Growing\nEconomy An Empowered\nSociety A Leading\nDestination A Sustainable\nVi", "source": "market/En-Annual Report-Vision2030-2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F1_Color_Trends_Blac-001", "title": "Color Trends: From Black to Color (F1)", "content": "Color Trends: From Black to Color (F1)\n\nText corpus: color keyword trends + season / scene matching rules.\n\n黑色相关：black abaya / classic black abaya / plain black abaya（长期稳定高基数）。\n\n彩色相关：colored abaya / colour abaya / pastel abaya（增长更快，尤其在春夏与节庆前）。\n\n结构相关：open abaya（与“彩色/层搭/内搭可见”高度绑定）。\n\n风格相关：designer abaya / modern abaya / minimal abaya（城市年轻化语境更常出现）。\n\n图案相关：printed abaya / digital print abaya / ombre abaya / gradient abaya（社交与出片导向）。\n\n高频穿着：黑色更耐脏，适合“易打理”叙事（抗皱、可机洗、色牢度）。\n\njet black / deep black / soft black / charcoal black\n\nmatte black / satin black / textured black\n\neveryday black / work black / occasi", "source": "market/F1_Color_Trends_Black_to_Color_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F1_Color_Trends_Blac-002", "title": "dark green abaya / forest green abaya", "content": "dark green abaya / forest green abaya\n\njewel tone abaya / deep tone abaya\n\n春夏白天：薄荷绿（mint）、天空蓝（sky blue）、雾粉（dusty pink）、奶油白（cream/ivory）更“清爽、年轻、出片”。\n\n节庆（Ramadan/Eid）前：浅色与粉彩更容易成为“新衣”选择；强调“清新、柔和、体面”。\n\n浅色合规底线：必须强调 opaque / fully lined / not see-through，尤其雪纺与轻薄面料。\n\npastel abaya / pastel color abaya\n\nbaby blue abaya / sky blue abaya\n\nivory abaya / off-white abaya / beige abaya\n\n社交与“出片”：渐变（ombre/gradient）和数码印花更容易形成记忆点，适合聚会、旅行、下午茶。\n\n“一件有重点”：当衣服已是印花/渐变时，配饰要收敛（纯色鞋包/简洁腰带），避免信息过载。\n\n印花类型匹配：花卉（floral）更偏春夏与白天；几何（geometric）更偏都市通勤；大理石纹/抽象（marble/abstract）更偏现代。\n\nprinted abaya / digital print abaya\n\nfloral print", "source": "market/F1_Color_Trends_Black_to_Color_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F1_Color_Trends_Blac-003", "title": "Color diversification + pastels + open abaya observation: https://agsi.org/analy", "content": "Color diversification + pastels + open abaya observation: https://agsi.org/analysis/saudi-arabias-abaya-signals-a-new-era-of-fashion-empowerment-and-cultural-pride/\n\nInternal trend signal (keyword growth): colored/pastel/open abaya rising (Google Trends framing) — referenced in existing corpus pages.\n\nExamples of printed/ombre abaya product language: https://mariam-col.com/products/elegant-ombre-gradient-abaya-gradient-print-fringed-robe-dress-abaya", "source": "market/F1_Color_Trends_Black_to_Color_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F2_Pattern_Styles_Mo-001", "title": "Text corpus: pattern → audience → scene → “not too flashy” boundaries.", "content": "Text corpus: pattern → audience → scene → “not too flashy” boundaries.\n\narabic geometric / arabesque geometric\n\ntone-on-tone geometric / subtle geometric\n\njacquard geometric / woven motif / self-pattern\n\nmonogram-like motif / micro pattern\n\nembossed texture / textured weave\n\n同色系（tone-on-tone）优先：黑底黑纹、深蓝底深蓝纹。\n\n纹样尺度：micro / small-scale 优先；大面积几何更容易“强存在感”。\n\nfloral print / botanical print / vine motif\n\nwatercolor floral / soft floral\n\nborder print / placement print（下摆/袖口定位花）\n\nembroidered floral (tone-on-tone) / applique floral\n\n花型大小：micro floral / small floral 更日常；大花更“节庆/度假”。\n\n配色数量：控制在 2–4 色更稳；多彩大花更", "source": "market/F2_Pattern_Styles_Modest_Boundaries_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F2_Pattern_Styles_Mo-002", "title": "颜色对比：黑+白 / 黑+米 / 深蓝+白最保守；荧光/高饱和更冒险。", "content": "颜色对比：黑+白 / 黑+米 / 深蓝+白最保守；荧光/高饱和更冒险。\n\n“黑底 + 细白条：干净利落，像‘workwear modest’。”\n\nGeometric jacquard examples (KSA retail): https://www.leem.com/sa/p/geometric-jacquard-abaya-s2510170\n\nSubtle geometric texture product language example: https://abay.com/products/textured-abaya-with-subtle-geometric-pattern-2\n\nColor-block abaya example (KSA retail): https://www.rivafashion.com/en-sa/color-block-abaya-with-hijab-2-pcs-101109-25033-088.html", "source": "market/F2_Pattern_Styles_Modest_Boundaries_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F3_Craft_Detail_Embe-001", "title": "Craft & Detail Embellishments Lexicon (F3)", "content": "Craft & Detail Embellishments Lexicon (F3)\n\nText corpus: element keywords for titles / tags / search (CN + EN + common variants).\n\n组合标签：黑色+金线 / open abaya+lace cuff / evening+sequin / street+metal hardware。\n\n检索扩展：同义词、拼写变体、常见电商词（如 \"beaded\", \"sequined\", \"jacquard\", \"cutwork\"）。\n\n袖口刺绣 / cuff embroidery / embroidered cuff / cuff detail\n\n袖口滚边 / cuff trim / cuff piping / sleeve hem detail\n\n肩部刺绣 / shoulder embroidery / embroidered shoulder\n\n领口刺绣 / neckline embroidery / collar embroidery\n\n门襟刺绣 / placket embroidery / front panel embroidery\n\n下摆刺绣 / hem embroidery / embroidered hem\n\n侧缝刺绣 / side seam embro", "source": "market/F3_Craft_Detail_Embellishments_Lexicon_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F3_Craft_Detail_Embe-002", "title": "金线刺绣 / gold thread embroidery / zari embroidery / metallic thread embroidery", "content": "金线刺绣 / gold thread embroidery / zari embroidery / metallic thread embroidery\n\n银线刺绣 / silver thread embroidery / metallic silver embroidery\n\n手工刺绣 / hand embroidery / hand-embroidered\n\n机绣 / machine embroidery / embroidered motif\n\n立体刺绣 / 3D embroidery / raised embroidery\n\n贴布绣 / appliqué / applique embroidery\n\n珠绣 / beaded embroidery / beadwork embroidery\n\n亮片绣 / sequin embroidery / sequined embroidery\n\n镂空绣 / cutwork embroidery / broderie anglaise (cotton eyelet)\n\n盘绳绣 / cord embroidery / soutache\n\n阿拉伯纹样刺绣 / arabesque embroidery / Islamic motif embroidery\n\n花卉刺绣 / floral embroidery / botanical embroid", "source": "market/F3_Craft_Detail_Embellishments_Lexicon_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F3_Craft_Detail_Embe-003", "title": "蕾丝拼接 / lace panel / lace insert / lace patchwork", "content": "蕾丝拼接 / lace panel / lace insert / lace patchwork\n\n蕾丝袖口 / lace cuff / lace sleeve hem\n\n网纱拼接 / mesh panel / tulle panel / net insert\n\n镂空 / cut-out / cutwork / openwork\n\n布罗德里镂空 / eyelet / broderie anglaise\n\n激光镂空 / laser cut / laser-cut details\n\n雕花镂空 / perforated pattern / punched cutwork\n\n不透里衬 / fully lined / with lining / full lining\n\n局部镂空 / subtle cut-out / small cutout / peekaboo detail\n\n内搭可遮 / with inner layer / underlay\n\n不露肤 / modest coverage / opaque finish / not see-through\n\n低透感 / low-sheer / minimal sheerness / controlled transparency\n\n亮片 / sequin / sequins / sequined\n\n微亮片 / micro sequin ", "source": "market/F3_Craft_Detail_Embellishments_Lexicon_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F3_Craft_Detail_Embe-004", "title": "珍珠点缀 / pearl embellishment / pearl beading", "content": "珍珠点缀 / pearl embellishment / pearl beading\n\n水晶点缀 / crystal embellishment / rhinestone detail\n\n手工钉珠 / hand-beaded / hand-applied beads\n\n珠链流苏 / beaded fringe / beaded tassel\n\n晚宴 / evening / evening abaya / night event\n\n婚礼 / wedding guest / bridal event / engagement\n\n礼服款 / occasion wear / formal abaya / party abaya\n\nsubtle shine / soft shimmer / gentle sparkle\n\nminimal bling / understated glam\n\nscattered accents (not all-over)\n\nmicro embellishment / fine beading\n\n金属扣 / metal buckle / buckle detail\n\n按扣 / snap button / snap closure\n\n金属纽扣 / metal button / decorative buttons\n\n拉链 / zipper / zip-front ", "source": "market/F3_Craft_Detail_Embellishments_Lexicon_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-F3_Craft_Detail_Embe-005", "title": "袖口扣 / cuff buckle / cuff button", "content": "袖口扣 / cuff buckle / cuff button\n\nblack abaya + tone-on-tone embroidery + cuff detail\n\nopen abaya + geometric jacquard + minimal trim\n\nEid abaya + floral embroidery + subtle shimmer\n\nevening abaya + micro sequin + pearl beading\n\nstreet abaya + metal buckle + zip detail + chain charm\n\nlace cuff + fully lined + low-sheer", "source": "market/F3_Craft_Detail_Embellishments_Lexicon_TextCorpus.docx", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
