    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-020", "title": "6 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdA", "content": "6 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nfollowing section examines these regional effects  \nin detail.\nRegional effects of the \nconflict in the Middle East\nThe most severe and immediate economic con -\nsequences of the conflict will be felt within the \nMENAAP region itself. The nature and magnitude \nof the impacts vary considerably across countries \nand sectors, shaped by two principal factors: (1) \nproximity to the conflict and (2) exposure to the \neconomic channels it has disrupted. \nAt the epicenter of the conflict are the Islamic \nRepu", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-021", "title": "7 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL ", "content": "7 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026  chAPte R 1\nmass displacement that the conflict brings—espe -\ncially when potential buffers, such as remittances \nfrom the Gulf, are at risk.\nOil and gas production and exports have been \nheavily impacted by the Strait of Hormuz closure\nOther countries at the epicenter of hostilities—\nBahrain, the Islamic Republic of Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, \nOman, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, and the United Arab \nEmirates—share two structural characteristics that \ndefine their exposure to the conflict’s economic fall -\nout. First, their e", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-022", "title": "8 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPd", "content": "8 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026\nFIGURE 1.4 Direct effects of the conflict through oil and gas exports\n0102030405060708090100Share of exports (percent)\nIraq\nKuwait\nBahrain\nSaudi Arabia\nUnited Arab\nEmirates\nQatarB. Share of oil and gas exports that traverses the Strait of\nHormuz, given alternative routes\n0102030405060708090Crude oil production transported through the Strait of Hormuz (percent)\nIraq\nIran, Islamic Rep.\nKuwait\nSaudi Arabia\nUnited Arab\nEmirates\nQatarA. Share of total oil production that traverses the Strait of \nHormuz", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-023", "title": "9 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL ", "content": "9 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026  chAPte R 1\nThe medium- and long-term picture is highly \nuncertain. A short-lived conflict that inflicts lim -\nited damage to infrastructure could allow output to \nrecover quickly once trade flows resume. Sustained \ndamage to production infrastructure—wells, pro -\ncessing and storage facilities, and pipelines—could \nimpair the ability to restore normal operations long \nafter hostilities cease, with scarring repercussions \nthat outlast the conflict itself.3 If the conflict were \nto protract, deteriorating perce", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-024", "title": "10 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uP", "content": "10 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026\nIncreasing energy and fertilizer prices pose \nrisks to balance of payments and food security\nThe increase in oil and gas prices translates to a \nnegative terms-of-trade shock across MENAAP oil importers, as average prices of imports become more expensive relative to exports. For oil import -\ners, persistently high oil and gas prices reduce the purchasing power of the economy, depressing pri-vate consumption and investment and hindering economic growth. Moreover, higher import bills can deepen exi", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-025", "title": "11 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APR", "content": "11 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026 chAPteR  1\nprices and a depreciating currency can be especially \ndamaging.\nA clear illustration of this dynamic emerged follow -\ning the Russian Federation’s invasion of Ukraine. \nInflation—food inflation in particular—hit devel -\noping economies in the region that experienced currency depreciation the hardest. Analysis suggests that, had their currencies remained stable, infla -\ntion in these countries would have been signifi-cantly more contained (Belhaj et al. 2022; Gatti et al. 2023). Economies operatin", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-026", "title": "12 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uP", "content": "12 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026\nConflict will affect tourism-dependent \ncountries in the region\nThe ongoing conflict has severely disrupted the \nregional airspace, with direct consequences for tour-ism—a major income source across the region. The magnitude of economic impact varies across coun -\ntries, as the degree of airspace disruption and the economy’s dependence on tourism revenues vary.\nDuring the first weeks of the conflict, flight dis -\nruptions were most pronounced at airports closest \nto active conflict, including tho", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-027", "title": "13 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APR", "content": "13 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026 chAPteR  1\nin neighboring countries—Jordan, Lebanon, and \nTürkiye. Afghanistan also looms large in the regional displacement picture: 2.9 million Afghans returned \nin 2025, primarily from the Islamic Republic of Iran (around 1.9 million) and Pakistan (around \n1 million). Pakistan is still hosting about 1.6 mil-\nlion Afghan refugees and asylum-seekers, while Iran hosts roughly 750,000 registered Afghan refugees on top of much larger populations of other Afghans with different legal statuses in both countries", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-028", "title": "14 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "14 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nSpillover risk to financial markets in \nMENAAP \nAssessing the full toll of the conflict on foreign \ndirect investment (FDI), balance of payments, \nand fiscal positions of the MENAAP economies \nremains difficult at this stage. These statistics are \nreported at quarterly or annual frequency and will \ntake months to appear in official sources. Certain \nhigh-frequency data from financial markets offer a \nmore immediate window into investor sentiment, \nsovereign risk, and capital flows—though they \nrequ", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
