    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-030", "title": "16 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uP", "content": "16 chAPteR  1 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026\nThe conflict—including targeted attacks on dig -\nital infrastructure—puts these gains at risk. Even \nafter a ceasefire, a sustained shift in risk perceptions could slow the return of investment flows, and the timeline for recovery will depend heavily on how quickly and credibly regional stability is restored. Given that FDI data from national accounts are updated less frequently, these effects would be reflected in the data with a lag.\nRemittances represent one of the most consequen -\ntial financ", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-031", "title": "17 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APR", "content": "17 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026 chAPteR  1\nscarring and the risks of prolonged \nconflict\nSome of the economic impacts of the current con -\nflict are immediate and could fade when hostilities \ncease. Some may outlast the conflict itself with medium- to long-term consequences. Many dis -\nruptions filter through economic channels with a lag. For example, rising fertilizer prices today could reduce crop yields and increase food prices next year, while elevated oil and gas prices will gradu-ally raise production costs through supply linkages. ", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-032", "title": "18 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "18 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nMacroeconomic outlook\nconflict exposure drives diverging \neconomic outlooks across menAAP\nThe April 2026 World Bank’s Macro Poverty \nOutlook forecasts that the region’s aggregate (exclud -\ning the Islamic Republic of Iran) GDP growth \nwill decelerate to 1.8  percent in 2026, down from \n4.0 percent estimated for 2025 (see table 1.1). The \n2026 forecast has been downgraded by 2.4  percent-\nage points since the January projections, reflecting \nthe adverse effects of the ongoing conflict. \nGrowth in th", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-033", "title": "19 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL", "content": "19 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026  chAPte R 1\nTABLE 1.1  Real GDP growth forecasts\nReal GDP growth (percent)\nApril 2026 forecasts Changes since January 2026 Changes since October 2025\n2024 2025e 2026f 2025e 2026f 2025e 2026f\nMENAAP (excl. the Islamic \nRepublic of Iran)2.4 4.0 1.8 0.3 −2.4 0.5 −2.4\n  MENA (excl. the Islamic \nRepublic of Iran)2.3 4.2 1.6 0.3 −2.7 0.5 −2.8\n  Gulf Cooperation Council 2.7 4.4 1.3 0.6 −3.1 0.9 −3.1\n  Qatar 3.0 3.2 −5.7 0.4 −11 .0 0.4 −11 .0\n  u nited Arab emirates 4.0 5.6 2.4 0.8 −2.7 0.8 −2.6\n  s audi Arabia 2.6 4", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-034", "title": "20 Chapter 1 MIDDLe eaSt, NOrth aFrICa, aFGhaNIStaN & paKIStaN eCONOMIC UpDate |", "content": "20 Chapter 1 MIDDLe eaSt, NOrth aFrICa, aFGhaNIStaN & paKIStaN eCONOMIC UpDate | aprIL 2026\nMorocco and Pakistan. Other risks of the conflict \ninclude softer remittances from the Gulf for Jordan and Pakistan, slower tourism in Jordan, potential disruptions in the Red Sea for Djibouti, and slower European market growth for Morocco. \nThe conflict is expected to severely stress Lebanon’s \nfragile economic recovery in 2025. Tourism is set to stall amid flight disruptions and displacement, while heightened uncertainty will weigh on con -\nsumption and investment. Rising oil prices could widen the cu", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-035", "title": "21 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL", "content": "21 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026  chAPte R 1\nincrease inflation, strain foreign exchange reserves, \nand force governments and firms to absorb much \nhigher fuel and input costs. Financial spillovers can \namplify these pressures further, as weaker investor \nconfidence leads markets to demand higher risk \npremia from all developing economies, pushing \nup sovereign spreads, borrowing costs, and cur -\nrency pressures, particularly in countries with exist -\ning fiscal or external vulnerabilities. The conflict’s \neconomic effects can spread well be", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-036", "title": "22 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "22 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nand strengthen modestly. However, the outlook is \nfraught with risks due to evolving security dynamics, \ninstitutional arrangements, and disruptions to nat -\nural gas supplies and electricity generation since the \nonset of the most recent conflict. In a highly uncer -\ntain regional context, investment pledges may be \nreprioritized, affecting prospects for external financ -\ning and reconstruction support. Return movements \nare expected to raise short-term pressures on public \nservices and humanitari", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-037", "title": "23 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APR", "content": "23 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026 chAPteR  1\nEvidence from major conflicts in Algeria, the West \nBank and Gaza, Iraq, the Republic of Yemen, and Syria shows a strikingly consistent pattern of eco -\nnomic deterioration.\n10 In the year immediately \nfollowing the onset of conflict, income per capita declined by an average of 12 percent relative to \npreconflict levels. Critically, these losses are not transitory: Seven years after the start of the con -\nflict, income per capita remains 24 percent below \nits preconflict level (figure 1.13). The ", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-038", "title": "24 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "24 chAPte R 1 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nANNEX 1A  Macro Poverty Outlook\nTABLE 1A.1  Real GDP per capita growth, inflation, current account balance, and fiscal account balance \nestimates and forecasts, 2025–26 \nReal GDP per capita \ngrowth (percent)Inflation (percent)Current account \nbalance  \n(percent of GDP)Fiscal account \nbalance  \n(percent of GDP)\n2025e 2026f 2025e 2026f 2025e 2026f 2025e 2026f\nMENAAP (excl. the Islamic Republic \nof Iran)1.8 0.1 1.8 3.0 1.8 3.3 −4.0 −2.9\n  MENA (excl. the Islamic Republic \nof Iran)2.6 0.1 1.4 2.9 2.1 4", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-039", "title": "25", "content": "25\nKEY MESSAGES\n•Over the past decade, the number of active industrial policies—government action expected to increase \nstrategic business activity—has increased more than threefold in high-income and middle-income MENAAP (Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan, and Pakistan) economies. \n•Industrial policy often operates through indirect and less visible channels in the region, not only through ministries and formal policy announcements but also through state-owned enterprises, sovereign wealth funds, and other quasi-public institutions.\n•Differences in fiscal space, government bandwidth, mark", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-040", "title": "26 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "26 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\nKEY MESSAGES  (continued )\n• Four case studies across the region in Dubai, the Arab Republic of Egypt, Morocco, and Pakistan highlight \nthe heterogeneity in industrial policy experiences. From these experiences, two main elements emerge: \nan effective institutional setup for public-private dialogue and a public workforce with the expertise to \nengage in that dialogue.\n• Industrial policy can help, but it cannot substitute for broader reform. Industrial policy is most likely to \nsucceed when it addr", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-041", "title": "27 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL", "content": "27 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026  chAPte R 2\nby weak institutions or close ties between political \nand business interests, industrial policy can be cap -\ntured, poorly targeted, or used in ways that reinforce \nexisting distortions.\nThe main conclusion is, therefore, an optimistically \ncautious one. Industrial policy can help MENAAP \ncountries diversify, build capabilities, and support \nstructural transformation, and the region offers \nimportant examples of partial success—from Moroc -\nco’s automotive industry to Dubai’s digital ecosystem. \nB", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-042", "title": "28 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "28 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\ncredentials. These technocrats should be shielded \nfrom politics—which can be difficult to achieve in the \nMENAAP region because political power and busi -\nness interests are closely intertwined. In addition to \nformal government institutions, such as ministries, a \nnumber of other entities can play roles in industrial \npolicy: (1) NDBs, (2) SOEs, (3) SWFs, (4) cluster  \ninitia  tives, (5) public-private dialogue and coordi -\nnation agencies, (6) innovation and research and \ndevelop  ment (R&D) age", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-043", "title": "29 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APR", "content": "29 mIddLe eA st, noR th AFRIc A, AFghAnI stAn & PA kIstAn economIc uPdA te | APRIL 2026 chAPteR  2\nBOX 2.1 Special economic zones drive growth in surrounding areas: Evidence from nighttime lights’ \nintensity\nEvidence suggests that special economic zones (SEZs) \nare linked to higher economic activity in nearby areas, as measured by growth in nighttime lights (figure B2.1.1). The analysis covers 132 SEZs in MENAAP countries and 1,821 SEZs in 22 other countries from a sample created by the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD) for which the year of estab-lishment—or, if unavaila", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-MENAAP_REU_April_202-044", "title": "30 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPd", "content": "30 chAPte R 2 mIddLe eAst, noRth AFRI cA, AF ghAnIstAn & PA kIstAn econom Ic uPdAte | APRIL 2026\ncampaigns and strategic partnerships to promote \nits diverse tourism offerings. It also enacted such \nregulatory reforms as streamlined visa procedures to \nlower entry barriers for foreign visitors and reduce \ntransaction costs associated with travel to Egypt. \nThese measures constitute indirect market access \nassistance by lowering regulatory and informational \nbarriers for foreign consumers to enhance interna -\ntional demand for Egypt’s tourism services.\nMarket incentives\nMarket incentives are po", "source": "market/MENAAP REU_April 2026.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-001", "title": "September 2025McKinsey Direct", "content": "September 2025McKinsey Direct\nMeet the new Saudi \nconsumer: Ten trends \nshaping a fast-evolving \nmarket\nThe Saudi consumer and retail market has huge growth potential.  \nCapturing it will depend on understanding how consumer profiles  \nand behavior are changing. \nby Abdellah Iftahy, Pamela Merlino, and Simon Land", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-002", "title": "Saudi Arabia’s ambitious program  to transform ", "content": "Saudi Arabia’s ambitious program  to transform \nthe economy and wider society appears to be \nhaving an effect on the country’s consumer and \nretail market. Five years ago, there was no such \nthing as a tourist visa,1 for example, and the \nentertainment sector was limited. Yet in 2023, \naround 27.5 million foreign tourists visited Saudi \nArabia2 and 20.0 million people attended Riyadh \nSeason 2023, an annual festival launched in 2019.3 \nToday, it’s one of many vibrant cultural and sporting \nfestivals held around the country each year. \nBut there is still potential to expand the nation’s \nconsum", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-003", "title": "Today, about 90 percent of expatriates are blue-collar ", "content": "Today, about 90 percent of expatriates are blue-collar \nworkers. However, efforts to attract a greater number \nof highly skilled workers and encourage foreign \ncompanies to establish their regional headquarters \nin Riyadh mean that the proportion could fall to \naround 80 percent by 2035, given current economic \nforecasts. In this scenario, there could be double the \nnumber of highly skilled (and high-earning) expatriates \nliving in the kingdom than in 2024 (Exhibit 2).8 \nIn terms of international tourists, recent data \nindicates that there may be many millions more \nvisiting Saudi Arabia by 20", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-004", "title": "(Exhibit 3).12 Such a shift could create challenges ", "content": "(Exhibit 3).12 Such a shift could create challenges \nfor companies whose products don’t appeal \nto youthful consumers or for those that cannot \nengage such consumers digitally. Conversely, \nthere could be considerable opportunities for \nthose able to win youthful consumers’ business. \nWomen at the forefront . The number of women in the \nworkforce will likely double to three million between \n2023 and 2035 and account for 37 percent of the \nworkforce.13 Of note, two-thirds of those women are expected to be in high-skill jobs, reflecting in part \nthat 50 percent of university graduates will be wo", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-005", "title": "Exhibit 3", "content": "Exhibit 3\nWeb <2025>\n<MCK241046 Perceptive on Consumer Retails>\nExhibit <3> of <5>\nSaudi Arabia local consumer base (population \n15 years old or older) by generations,1 % (million)Young consumers will likely control 77 percent of household income \nby 2035.\n1The sizes of generations are estimated based on the 2022 GASTAT growth rates and age-speci/f_ic death rates. These estimates are adjusted for 2022–35 in \naccordance with United Nations estimations. Share of people 65 years old or older in population will increase from 3% in 2023 to 9% in 2035. \n2Compensation of employees is distributed base", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-006", "title": "could be rapid. A year ago, it was difficult to buy a ", "content": "could be rapid. A year ago, it was difficult to buy a \nready-made meal anywhere. Today, these products \nhave a growing presence on the shelves of big \nsupermarkets. \nFurther, in line with trends observed in other \nmarkets, a higher proportion of working women may \nlead to smaller families. In the past two decades, \nthe average size of the Saudi household has fallen \nfrom six to five.15 This pace of decline could continue \nas more women join the workforce: Within little \nmore than a decade, the average Saudi family could \nconsist of four people, which may result in families \nbuying smaller quan", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-007", "title": "Exhibit 4", "content": "Exhibit 4\nWeb <2025>\n<MCK241046 Perceptive on Consumer Retails>\nExhibit <4> of <5>\nRegional population distribution based on natural growth and announced development \nprojects (not exhaustive),1 millionMega infrastructure projects will lead the Saudi population to converge \naround hubs.\nNote: The boundaries and names shown on maps do not imply oﬃcial endorsement or acceptance by McKinsey & Company.\n1Total population is expected to reach 41 million to 44 million by 2035. Regional distribution of growth is in line with 2010–22 historical growth, adjusted to \nannounced megaprojects. Megaprojects ", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-008", "title": "Zero boundaries. Saudis are among the world’s most ", "content": "Zero boundaries. Saudis are among the world’s most \ndigitally connected people,23 indicating the potential \nof what remains a relatively underdeveloped B2C \ne-commerce market (for the time being). Our \nresearch suggests that e-commerce could grow by \nan average of 15 percent per annum to reach $60 \nbillion by 2035.24 But Saudi consumers are already \nmaking it clear that they prefer an omnichannel \nexperience. Previous McKinsey surveys show that \nsome 70 percent of Saudi consumers already use \nmultiple channels when it comes to searching for \nand buying accessories, apparel, footwear, and \njewe", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-009", "title": "In Saudi Arabia, consumers often change retailers ", "content": "In Saudi Arabia, consumers often change retailers \nas part of trading down: 53 percent of those who \ntraded down changed retailers for a lower price and \n22 percent changed brands for a lower price or a \nprivate label.31 New Saudi consumers will likely be just \nas keen to experiment, so now is the time to begin \nshaping their future choices. \nConscious consumer . Saudi consumers are \nbeginning to change their consumption behavior \nbecause of concerns about sustainability. Previous \nMcKinsey research showed this was the case for \n40 percent of Saudi consumers, compared with \n46 percent globally", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Meet_the_new_Saudi_c-010", "title": "Capturing the full growth potential that lies within ", "content": "Capturing the full growth potential that lies within \nthe Saudi consumer and retail market will depend \non embedding certain performance drivers withing \nthe organization—innovation and digital and \nanalytics capabilities, for example. However, the \nstarting point is to understand and keep track of \nfast-shifting consumer trends. Serving the new \nSaudi consumer well will depend upon it. \nCopyright © 2025 McKinsey & Company. All rights reserved.Abdellah Iftahy  is a leader of the Consumer Packaged Goods and Retail Practices in the Middle East and a senior partner \nin McKinsey’s Dubai office, Pa", "source": "market/Meet the new Saudi consumer. Ten trends shaping a fast-evolving market.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-001", "title": "Methodology and Quality  Report of  Consumer Price ", "content": "Methodology and Quality  Report of  Consumer Price \nIndex   \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nV-3.1 \nQuality Management", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-002", "title": "2 ", "content": "2 \n \n \n \nTable of Contents         \n1. Contact  ................................ ................................ ................................ ................................ .................  5 \n1.1. Contact organization  ................................ ................................ ................................ ..................  5 \n1.2. Contact organization unit  ................................ ................................ ................................ ..........  5 \n1.3. Contact person function  ................................ ................................ .......", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-003", "title": "3 ", "content": "3 \n \n8. Frequency of dissemination  ................................ ................................ ................................ ............  11 \n9. Accessibility and clarity  ................................ ................................ ................................ ...................  11 \n9.1. News release  ................................ ................................ ................................ .............................  11 \n9.2. Publications  ................................ ................................ ................................ ......................", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-004", "title": "4 ", "content": "4 \n \n17. Statistical processing  ................................ ................................ ................................ ...................  17 \n17.1.  Source data  ................................ ................................ ................................ ............................  17 \n17.2.  Frequency of data collection  ................................ ................................ ...............................  18 \n17.3.  Data collection ................................ ................................ ................................ .......................  18 ", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-005", "title": "5 ", "content": "5 \n \n1. Contact  \n1.1. Contact organization  General Authority for Statistics  \n1.2. Contact organization  unit  Price Statistics  Department  \n1.3. Contact person function  Director of Price Statistics Department  \n1.4. Contact mail address  P.O. Box: 3735  \nRiyadh, 11481  \nKingdom of Saudi Arabia  \n1.5. Contact email address  info@stats.gov.sa  \n1.6. Contact phone number  199009  \n \n2. Methodology and Quality  Update  \n2.1. Methodology and Quality  last update  01/12/2024  \n \n \n3. Statistical Presentation  \n3.1. Data description   \nThe Consumer Price Index covers data related to consumer pri", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-006", "title": "6 ", "content": "6 \n \n• Provide statistics on retail price fluctuations of goods and services in the consumer \nbasket.  \n• Provide statistics on consumer price index across a time series of statistical data.  \n• Contribute to satisfying Vision 2030 objectives, as well as regional needs such as \nthose set by the GGCC -STAT and international requirements set by the \nInternational Monetary Fund.  \nThe data is further used to estimate:  \n• Indices and variation rates by category of expenditure, (for reference month) . \n• Indices by city and expenditure category, (for reference month) . \n• Annual variation rate by ", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-007", "title": "7 ", "content": "7 \n \nThe classifications are available on the GASTAT’s website : www.stats.gov.sa  \n3.3. Sector coverage  \nThe Consumer Price Index covers the following main economic activities:   \n• Food products, beverages, tobacco, textiles.  \n• Retail.  \n• Restaurants and hotels.  \n3.4. Statistical concepts and definitions  \nTerminologies and concepts of Consumer Price Index : \n• Index:  \nA rational number that measures variation in one or more phenomena. It is calculated by \ndividing its face value at the comparison period by its value at the base period.  \n• Consumer Price Index:  \nA consumer price inde", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-008", "title": "8 ", "content": "8 \n \nThe percentage of expenditure on certain goods or services compared to the total \nexpenditure on goods and services inside the consumer basket.  \n• Points of Sale:  \nEstablishments and units from which the prices of goods and services that are included in \nthe goods and services basket, are collected. For example, retail shops such as grocery \nstores, clothing shops, and other kinds of shops like the ones that provide do ctors\' \nservices, as well as rental units (apartment, villa, a floor of a villa, old house), and other \nservice shops.  \n• Shadow Sample:  \nIt is a set of backup points o", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-009", "title": "9 ", "content": "9 \n \n3.7. Reference area  \nThe survey covers sixteen  cities: Riyadh, Mecca, Jeddah, Taif, Medina, Buraidah, Dammam, \nHofuf, Abha, Tabuk, Hail, Arar, Jizan, Najran, Al -Baha, and Al -Jouf. These cities represent all \nthirteen  administrative regions in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia.  \n3.8. Time coverage  \nData is available from 2013 to the current  month . \n3.9. Base period  \n2018 is the base year used for calculating the consumer price index.  \n \n4. Unit of measure  \nSome results are reported as a percentage (EX. Monthly percentage of change and annual \npercentage of change of the indices.  \n \n", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-010", "title": "10 ", "content": "10 \n \n6. Confidentiality  \n6.1. Confidentiality - policy  \nAccording to the Royal Decree No. 23 dated 07 -12-1397, data must always be kept \nconfidential, and must be used by GASTAT only for statistical purposes.  \nTherefore, the data are protected in the data servers of the Authorit y. \n6.2. Confidentiality - data treatment  \nData are displayed in appropriate tables to facilitate its summarization, comprehension, results \nextraction , comparison with other data and coming up with  statistical connotations  for the \nstudy community. It is also easier to check tables without the need to see the", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-011", "title": "11 ", "content": "11 \n \n7.3. User access  \nOne of GASTAT’s objectives is to better meet its clients\' needs, so it immediately provides \nthem with the publication’s  results once the Consumer Price Index publication  is published.  \nIt also receives questions and inquiries from  the clients about the Publication  and its results \nthrough various communication channels, such as:  \n• GASTAT’s official website : www.stats.gov.sa  \n• GASTAT’s official e -mail address : info@stats.gov.sa  \n• Client Support’s e -mail address : cs@stats.gov.sa  \n• Official visits to GASTAT’s official head office in Riyadh or one of its", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-012", "title": "12 ", "content": "12 \n \n9.2. Publications  \nGASTAT issues the Consumer Price Index’s publications and reports on a regular basis within a \npre-prepared publishing plan and installed on the GASTAT’s website. GASTAT is keen to \npublish its publications in a manner that serves all users of different type s, including \npublications in different formats that contain (publication tables, data graphs, indicators, \nMethodology and Quality  Report , and used questionnaires) in both English and Arabic.  \n Results of Consumer Price Index are available a t: https://www.stats.gov.sa/ar/394  \n9.3. On-line database  \nThe data", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-013", "title": "13 ", "content": "13 \n \nGASTAT\'s secure platform, \" Etaha ,\" while more sensitive datasets are accessible only through \nsecure physical lab environments managed by GASTAT.  \n9.5. Other  \nNot available.  \n9.6. Documentation on methodology  \nThe consumer price index framework is based on the consumer price index manual  issued in \n2004 by the International Monetary Fund in cooperation with the International Labor \nOrganization, the European Statistical Office, the United Nations, and the World Bank, and the \nconsumer price index manual  issued in 2020 by the International Monetary Fund.  \nhttps://www.stats.gov.sa", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-014", "title": "14 ", "content": "14 \n \n10.2.  Quality assessment  \nGASTAT  performs all statistical activities according to a national model (Generic Statistical \nBusiness Process Model – GSBPM). According to the GSBPM, the final phase of statistical \nactivities is overall evaluation using information gathered in each phase or sub -process. This \ninformation is used to prepare the evaluation report which outlines all the quality issues  \nrelated to the specific statistical activity and serves as input for improvement actions.  \n \n11. Relevance  \n11.1.  User needs   \nInternal GASTAT’s users , who  make use of Consumer Price In", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-015", "title": "15 ", "content": "15 \n \n11.3.  Completeness  \nThe Consumer Price Index data is based in its data on the field survey of the Consumer Price \nIndex, a survey conducted by the General Authority for Statistics on a monthly basis and \nclassified as (economic statistics) in which prices are collected through field visits to points of \nsale and the provision of services at retail prices in various administrative regions in the \nKingdom of Saudi Arabia, as well as the completion of electronic questionnaires containing a \nnumber of goods and servic es, through which indicators related to the consumer price index \nare 14", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-016", "title": "16 ", "content": "16 \n \non a monthly basis, and with a delay of not more than month after the end of the reference \nperiod. If the data are from different source, they may be published in a different frequency.  \n13.2.  Punctuality  \nPublication takes place in accordance with published release dates for the Consumer Price \nIndex in the GASTAT webpage. The data are available at the expected time, as scheduled the \nrelease calendar, If the publication delayed the reasons would be provided.  \n \n14. Coherence and comparability  \n14.1.  Comparability – geographical  \nData are fully comparable.  \n14.2.  Comparability", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-017", "title": "17 ", "content": "17 \n \n14.4.  Coherence - internal  \nConsumer Price Index estimates for a given reference period have full internal coherence, as \nthey are all based on the same corpus of microdata,  and they are calculated using the same \nestimation methods.  \n \n15. Resource s used   \nDescription  Total  \nTotal staff (GASTAT’s staff , researcher s). 130 \nNumber of unites surveyed . 15120  \nTotal days of data collection  period  (end dat e – start date) . 21 \nAverage conducted  interviewer per day  (during data collection) . 720 \n \n16. Data revision  \n16.1.  Data revision - policy  \nNot applicable, only final ", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-018", "title": "18 ", "content": "18 \n \nthe use of administrative records from the Ejar platform, covering the 16 cities mentioned \nearlier . \nThe main published variants of the survey data are:  \n• Consumer Price Indices by cities and expenditure categories.  \n• Consumer Price Indices and monthly percentage changes.  \n• Consumer Price Indices and annual percentage changes.  \n17.2.  Frequency of data collection  \nMonthly . \n17.3.  Data collection  \nData collection from survey:  \nThe Consumer Price Index data collection is carried out through Computer -Assisted Personal \nInterviews (CAPI) . And it is saved on the Authority’s da", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Methodology_and_Qual-019", "title": "19 ", "content": "19 \n \nIn the Consumer Price Index , interviewers collect from respondents, a detailed description of \neach field . This information is then coded in -house by an automated process, which is \nreviewed by a small -dedicated team of coding experts using a series of consistency checks.  \nData E diting : \nSpecialists of  Price Statistics Department  have processed and analyzed data in this stage, and \nthis step was based on the following measures:  \n• Sort and arrange data in groups or different categories in a serial order.  \n• Summarize detailed data into main points or main data . \n• Linking bet", "source": "market/Methodology and Quality Report of Consumer Price Index_ EN.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-001", "title": "Voice of the ", "content": "Voice of the \nConsumer 2024: \nRegional findings \nEnsuring retail success in the \nMiddle East: \nTrust, innovation, and climate \nconsciousness", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-002", "title": "Executive Summary ", "content": "Executive Summary \nTrust may be intangible, but it is a crucial motivation in consumer purchase decisions. Trust is \nnot just a value; it is a strategic asset essential for business success. Consumers trust brands \nthey love, value organisations that use their data responsibly and are loyal to businesses that \naddress environmental concerns. With the rise of GenAI, trust is even more pivotal, as \nconsumers need reassurance about data security and ethical AI usage. Trusted brands benefit \nfrom greater consumer engagement, enhanced personalisation in sales and strong brand \nadvocacy. \nTo sustain", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-003", "title": "Trust and data protection ", "content": "Trust and data protection \nIn the region, trust is highest for the airline and healthcare industries, while social media companies rank the \nlowest. \nA critical factor for earning consumer trust is the responsible protection and use of their data. \nEconomic and environmental concerns \nFor regional consumers, inflation ranks as the number-one risk that consumers think could impact their country \nover the next year. \nClimate change and macroeconomic volatility are also seen as the top threats, with nearly half of the consumers \nsurveyed indicating that extreme weather conditions pose a huge risk", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-004", "title": "In this report we spotlight the perspectives of three distinct consumer types . ", "content": "In this report we spotlight the perspectives of three distinct consumer types . \nRight-click each picture to open its report in a new tab \nVoice of the \ntech-savvy \nconsumer \n+\nVoice of the \nclimate-conscious \nconsumer \n+\nVoice of the \nempowered \nconsumer \n+", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-005", "title": "Action points for retailers ", "content": "Action points for retailers \nIn the region’s dynamic and transforming retail environment, there is a paradox in growing \nconsumer spend and people’s distrust in certain businesses. Middle Eastern consumers, known \nfor their digital savviness and strong preference for technology-enhanced shopping \nexperiences, demand more from retailers and consumer companies. They prioritise data \nprotection, value transparency, and seek personalised and sustainable products. To succeed in \nthis competitive market, retailers must address these needs comprehensively. Companies must \nknow how to build and retain", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-006", "title": "Prioritise data protection and transparency 1", "content": "Prioritise data protection and transparency 1\nRetailers should invest in advanced data \nsecurity technologies and regularly update \nconsumers on data protection practices. \nTransparent communication about data \nusage and providing options for consumers \nto control their data will help build trust and \nloyalty among consumers. Despite the significant impact of AI, \nretailers must ensure all safety \nmeasures are in place to maintain trust. \nRetailers can invest in robust \ncybersecurity practices to build trust and \nalleviate digital security concerns, such \nas spam and phishing. They must also a", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-007", "title": "Emphasise sustainability in product offerings 3", "content": "Emphasise sustainability in product offerings 3\nRetailers should expand their range of \nsustainable products, highlighting \neco-friendly attributes in marketing and \npackaging. Offering locally produced goods \nand promoting products with a low \nenvironmental impact will resonate well \nwith these consumers. Retailers must increase their range of \nhealth-focused and sustainable food products, \nproviding clear nutritional information and \npromoting the benefits of these choices. \nHighlighting locally sourced and environmentally \nfriendly products will appeal to the \nhealth-conscious consumer segm", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-PwC_Voice_of_Consume-008", "title": "Norma Taki ", "content": "Norma Taki \nPartner, Middle East \nConsumer Markets \nLeader \nPwC Middle East \nnorma.taki@pwc.com Roy Hintze \nPartner, Middle \nEast Consumer \nMarkets \nPwC Middle East \nImad Matar \nPartner, Middle East \nTransaction Services \nLeader \nPwC Middle East \nimad.matar@pwc.com Maye Ayoub \nPartner, Middle East \nConsumer Markets \nPwC Middle East \nmaye.ayoub@pwc.com \nContact us \nContributors \nRazan Sawalha \nConsumer Markets \nMarketing Manager \nPwC Middle East \nrazan.sawalha@pwc.com Esha Nag \nLead Editor and Writer - \nThought leadership, \ninsights and reports \nPwC Middle East \nesha.nag@pwc.com \nBana Al-Majali", "source": "market/PwC_Voice_of_Consumer_Main_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-001", "title": "V ", "content": "V \nstats.gov.sa Saudi Youth  Statistics  \nReport  2023\n\nSaudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n2  \n \nTable of Contents  \n \n  \n03 Introduction  \n04 Sources  \n05 Terminology  \n06 Demographic Characteristics of Youth  \n10 Youth Health and Sports  \n13 Youth Education and Culture  \n16 Youth and Labor Market  \n20 Youth and Technology Usage  \n21 Youth and Property Ownership  \n23 Conclusion", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-002", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n3  \n \nIntroduction  \n\"Our youth are conscious, strong, educated, creative, and possess high values  \" \nThe Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, in its Vision 2030, emphasized investing in human resources and the \ngreat expectations from Saudi youth in the country\'s development and their pivotal role in achieving the \nVision\'s objectives. Stemming from the General Authori ty for Statistics\' (GASTAT) mission to provide \nupdated statistical products and services with added value characterized by accuracy, \ncomprehensiveness, and credibility that meet the requirements of ", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-003", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n4  \n \n   Sources  \n1. Census 2022 - General Authority for Statistics  \n2. Mid-year Population Estimates 2023 - General Authority for Statistics    \n3. Labor Market Statistics Survey (2022 -2023) - General Authority for Statistics    \n4. Household Culture and Entertainment Survey 2023 - General Authority for \nStatistics    \n5. Household Sports Practice Survey 2021 - General Authority for Statistics    \n6. Information and Communication Technology Access and Usage Survey for \nHouseholds and Individuals 2021 - General Authority for Statistics    \n7. Ministry o", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-004", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n5 Terminology  \n \n  Population  All individuals residing regularly in the Kingdom at the time of conducting the \ncensus or survey, including Saudi and non -Saudi nationals  \nAge Group  A segment of the population within a specific age range  \nYouth  Individuals aged between (15 -34 years)  \nGender  The individual\'s gender , being male or female  \nHead of Household  The person whom the family considers as its head and is not less than 15 years \nold \nAge-Specific \nFertility Rate  The ratio of annual births to women in a specific age or age group to the numbe", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-005", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n6 1.Demographic Characteristics of Youth     \n1.1. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Population by Age Groups  \nSaudi population under the age of 35 represents the highest proportion among age groups. According to \nthe 2022 Census and mid -year estimates for 2023, this group represents approximately 71% of the total \nSaudi population, indicating that Saudi society is y oung and vibrant, which is considered a positive \nindicator of Saudi society\'s ability to grow and develop.   \n  Source: Census 2022 and Estimates 2023   \n1.2. Percentage Distribution of Saud", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-006", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n7 1.3. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth by Administrative Region  \nSaudi youth are distributed among administrative regions in varying proportions ranging between 33.2% \nto 37. 8% for the years 2022 and 2023, as shown in the chart below.  \n \nSource: Census 2022 and Estimates 2023     \n \n1.4. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth According to Age Groups and \nGender   \nAccording to the percentage distribution of age groups for Saudi youth, the proportions of males and \nfemales are close to each other for each age group for the years 2022 and 2023. The", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-007", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n8 1.5. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth by Marital Status  \nSource: Census 2022      \nMarital status data for Saudi youth indicates that the largest segment is single youth, representing 65.3 %, \nwhile married youth represent 32.3 %. \n \n1.6. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth by Marital Status and Gender  \nThe percentage of married male youth was 25.3% and for females 39. 4%, while the percentage of single \nmale youth was 73. 7% and for females 56.8%, as shown in the chart below.  \n \nSource: Census 2022  \n  %65.3%32.3%2.2Percentage Distribution o", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-008", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n9 1.7. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth Heads of Households by \nAdministrative Region  \nThe proportions of Saudi youth heads of households vary from one region to another, reaching 35.8% in \nAl-Jouf region and 34.6% in the Northern Borders region, while the percentage of heads of households in \nAl-Baha region was 27.5% and 27. 7% in Al -Qassim region.  \n \nSource: Census 2022  \n  32.9 \n29.4 30.2 \n27.7 32.8 \n29.7 33.6 \n29.2 34.6 \n30.0 34.3 \n27.5 35.8 \nRiyadh Makkah Madinah Qassim Eastern\nRegionAseer Tabuk Hail Northern\nBordersJazan Najran Al-Baha Al-Jo", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-009", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n10 2. Youth Health and Sports  \n2.1. Age -Specific Fertility Rates for Young Saudi Females  \nThe age -specific fertility rate for young Saudi females reached 1.8%. The chart below shows the fertility \nrate according to the age distribution of young Saudi females.  \n \nSource: Census 2022  \n2.2. Saudi Youth Covered by Private Sector Health Insurance Policies by \nGender   \nSaudi youth are distributed across health fields according to main occupations, where females in nursing \nrepresent 75.48% compared to males in the same profession who represent 24.52%. In ", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-010", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n11 2.3. Saudi Youth Working in Health Fields by Occupation and Gender  \nSaudi youth are distributed across health fields according to main occupations, where females in nursing \nrepresent 75. 5% compared to males in the same profession who represent 24.5%. In allied health \nprofessions, males represent 63. 9% while females represent 36.1%. In pharmacy, the proportions \nbetween males and females are close, as shown in the chart below.   \n \nSource: Ministry of Health 2022      \n*The workforce of the Ministry of Health only and does not include other governme", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-011", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n12 2.5. Saudi Youth Deaths from Total Traffic Accident Deaths During Past Years  \nIn recent years, death rates due to traffic accidents among Saudi youth (aged 18 -30 years) have \nfluctuated within close percentages, reaching their lowest decrease in 2020 at 44% since 2017.  \n \nSource: Ministerial Committee for Traffic Safety  \n*Note:  Age group classification adopted by the General Traffic Department (18 -30 years)  \n \n  4243444546\n2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022Percentage of Saudi Youth Deaths from Total Traffic Accident Deaths During Past Years", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-012", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n13 3. Youth Education and Culture  \n3.1.   Percentage Distribution of Students in General Education by Gender  and \nEducational Level  \n \nSource: Ministry of Education, 2022  \n \n3.2.  Percentage Distribution of Students in Higher Education by Gender  and \nEducational Level  \n \nSource: Ministry of Education, 2022  \n3.3. Saudi Gifted Youth by Type of Achievement (2022 -2023)  \nThe number of gifted Saudi youth who participated with their achievements locally, regionally, or \ninternationally reached 504 young men and women. The National Olympiad for Scientific", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-013", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n14 3.4. Percentage Distribution of Cultural Events and Activities Venues Visited by \nSaudi Youth by Gender  \nCultural events and activities venues visited by Saudi youth within the Kingdom varied as shown in the \nchart below, with cinemas accounting for the largest share at approximately 30%, followed by national \ncelebration venues at 23%, while the remaining perc entages were distributed among other venues such \nas holiday festivals and traditional folk festivals.   \nSource: Household Culture and Entertainment Survey, 2023  \n  15.45.24.03.13.12.46.46.17.", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-014", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n15 3.5. Percentage Distribution of Cultural Activities practiced During the Past 12 \nMonths by Saudi Youth by Gender  \nAccording to the percentage distribution of cultural activities, these activities varied in practice by youth, \nwith females showing higher participation in activities such as fine arts (11.1%) and photography (10. 3%), \nwhile males showed higher participation in photography (7.6%) and writing (6. 4%).  \n \nSource: Household Culture and Entertainment Survey, 2023  \n3.6. Percentage Distribution of Recreational and Sports Activities Practiced", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-015", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n16 4. Youth and Labor Market  \n4.1. Percentage Distribution of Employed Saudi Youth by Gender  \nBased on labor market statistics for 2023, the percentage of employed females reached 31.8%, an \nincrease of 2.1% from 2022, while the percentage of employed males decreased in 2023 by 2.1% from \n2022, as shown in the chart below . \n \nSource: Labor Market Statistics Survey  \n \n4.2. Percentage Distribution of Employed Saudi Youth from Total Population in \nAge Group (15 -34 years) by Gender  \nThe percentage of employed Saudi youth in 2023 reached 41% of the total ", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-016", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n17 4.3. Saudi Youth Working in the Private Sector by Position and Gender   \nThe Kingdom\'s Vision focused on involving the private sector in the development process across various \nfields, including employing Saudi youth and enabling them to hold leadership positions. During 2022, the \nnumber of youth who held administrative position s in the private sector reached 27,702, and in 2023 \ntheir number reached 51,611, as shown in the chart below.  \n \nSource: Ministry of Human Resources and Social Development 2022  \n \n4.4. Labor Force Participation Rate for Saud", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-017", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n18 4.5. Unemployment Rate for Saudi Youth by Gender  \nThe unemployment rate for Saudi youth decreased in 2023 (12.9) by 1.6 from 2022. In 2023, the male \nunemployment rate reached 7.7 while the female rate reached 22.3, lower than 2022 as shown in the \nchart below.  \n \nSource: Labor Market Statistics Survey    \n4.6. Percentage Distribution of Unemployed Saudi Youth by Gender   \nThe percentage distribution of unemployed Saudi youth during 2023 shows that the percentage of \nunemployed females decreased by 4.9% from 2022, from 66.5% to 61.6% in 2023, while it", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-018", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n19 4.7. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth of Working Age by Gender   \nLabor market statistics showed that the percentage of Saudi youth of working age for males was 50.4% \nin 2022 and 49.8% in 2023, while for females it was 49.6% in 2022 and 50.2% in 2023. It is noted that \nit decreased for males in 2023 while increasing for f emales.   \nSource: Labor Market Statistics Survey  \n \n4.8. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth Outside the Labor Force by \nGender   \nThe results of the Labor Market Statistics Survey for 2022 -2023 showed a decrease in 2023 b", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-019", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n20 5. Youth and Technology Usage  \n5.1. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth by Internet Usage Areas (During \nthe Last 3 Months of 2021) by Gender   \nSource: Survey of Individuals and Households ICT Access and Usage 2021  \nSocial media participation captured the highest percentage among the top four areas of internet usage \nby youth (15 -34 years), reaching 94.9% for males and 94% for females, while downloading programs and \napplications had the lowest percentage among them at 84.9% for males and 83.2% for females.  \n5.2. Percentage Distribution of Saudi", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-020", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n21  \n6. Youth and Property Ownership    \n6.1.  Number of Saudi Youth Benefiting from Housing Support Provided by the \nReal Estate Development Fund by Type of Housing Product  \n \nSource: Ministry of Municipalities and Housing  \nWith the support provided by the state to youth, Saudi youth of both genders received housing support \nfrom the Real Estate Development Fund for real estate products including (villa/townhouse/apartment), \nwhere the number of males receiving this support reached 161,519 and females (5,296).   \n6.2. Percentage Distribution of Young Sa", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-021", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n22 6.3. Percentage Distribution of Saudi Youth Business Owners by \nEstablishment Size  and Gender   \n \n      Source: Ministry of Commerce  \nAs part of the state\'s support for youth, enabling them to work and manage their own establishments \nthrough financial and technical support and guidance provided by relevant authorities, the number of \nyouth (male and female) who own establishments accordin g to establishment size \n(micro/small/medium/large) increased from 1,659,192 in 2022 to 1,690,269 in 2023.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 62.7 63.1\n37.3 36.970.1 69.", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-022", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics Report  2023  \n23  \nConclusion  \n \nYouth in Saudi Arabia represent the largest demographic group and are the cornerstone of development \nand progress in society. In this context, we emphasize the vision of His Royal Highness Prince Mohammed \nbin Salman bin Abdulaziz, Crown Prince and Prime Minister, who highlights the vital role of youth in \nshaping the future of th e Kingdom. He expressed his optimistic outlook on the future driven by Saudi \nyouth, encapsulated in his memorable words : “Our youth are aware, strong, educated, creative, and uphold \nhigh values  ”. \nWe hop", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Youth_Statisti-023", "title": "Saudi Youth Statistics ", "content": "Saudi Youth Statistics \nReport   2023", "source": "market/Saudi Youth Statistics  en.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-001", "title": "Snap Consumer ARSaudi Arabia Report 20211", "content": "Snap Consumer ARSaudi Arabia Report 20211\nThe following report is a deep dive into how Snapchatters are using AR in Saudi Arabia based on the Snap Consumer AR Global Report.   For a more comprehensive market view, please read the Snap Consumer AR Global Report.", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-002", "title": "AR is a critical tool for brands to stand out  and deliver customer value and co", "content": "AR is a critical tool for brands to stand out  and deliver customer value and confidence.IntroductionTable of  Contents\n2AR’s Impact on BrandsAR is Evolving FastAR is Here Today and Here to StayAR is the next form of mobile engagement, elevating the consumer experience and setting new expectations for digital engagement.\nAR cannot be ignored as the new, rapidly-growing consumer experience that will soon be everywhere, all the time.AR is already here, widely recognized as both fun  and useful and driving fast adoption and growth.Conclusion0102030405There is an imperative to meet consumer’s AR d", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-003", "title": "1: See additional methodology details in appendix 2: 2021 Global Deloitte Digita", "content": "1: See additional methodology details in appendix 2: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissioned by Snap Inc. Base = Aggregate average (n=808) Q: AR Recognition for 9 examples 3: Base = Snapchatters (n=291) Q: Why do you use AR? 4: Base = Aggregate (n=840) Q: Augmented Reality is useful / Augmented Reality is not useful | A: Agree much more with A, Agree somewhat more with A5: Harvard Business Review Article, “How AR is Redefining Retail in the Pandemic”56% of Snapchatters in Saudi Arabia and across generations use AR to have fun; the majority are discovering AR through social / communicat", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-004", "title": "There is something really unique happening right now – an incredible number of h", "content": "There is something really unique happening right now – an incredible number of hobbyists and professionals are using AR. Downloading tools across the Snap AR platform. They’re taking time to learn, they’re curious.” \n1: See additional methodology details in appendix  *Global population of Gen Z and Millennials includes people ages 13-44 (based on UN World Population Prospects 2019) who have smartphones (based on Statista Digital Market Outlook) Note: 2021 AR Consumers based on people who use AR weekly or daily from 2021 Global Deloitte Study commissioned by Snap Inc.  2022 to 2025 forecasted i", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-005", "title": "Age DifferencesSnapchattersYounger people are 28% more likely to believe AR  is ", "content": "Age DifferencesSnapchattersYounger people are 28% more likely to believe AR  is important in their lives.1\n1: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissioned by Snap Inc. Base = Age 13-24 (n=349), Age 35-44 (n=166) Q: AR is important to me / AR is not important to me | A: Agree much more with A, Agree somewhat more with A 2: Publicis Groupe & Snap Inc. Study Base: Gen Z (n=1,261), Millennials (n=1,632), Gen X (n=1,107) QP7A: Thinking about your expected use of AR technology post-COVID, do you expect to start using AR? Please select one response 3: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissione", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-006", "title": "7", "content": "7\nAllan Cook Digital Reality Business Leader Deloitte Digital\nAs AR evolves, it will revolutionize our lives and will become as  significant of a technology shift as the web or mobile was to society, changing how we view and interact with the world around us.”\n\nconsumers96%A lot of people are using AR  to make purchase decisions… and plan to keep doing so \n9There are100 millionof Snapchatters agree they\'ll  use AR the same or more when shopping next year.2shopping with AR online  and in-stores.1\n1: Gartner Press Release2: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissioned by Snap Inc.  Base = Sna", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-007", "title": "AR captures consumer  attention", "content": "AR captures consumer  attention\nSnapchatters who frequently  use AR with their family and  friends are 23% more likely  to pay attention to a brand.2 leading to improved memories and more powerful responses from consumers.1AR delivers almost  2x the levels of visual attention compared to their non-AR equivalent, \nAR\n1: Zappar Article, “How augmented reality affects the brain”2: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissioned by Snap Inc. Base = Snapchatters with over 60% of friends and family sharing AR (n=51), Snapchatters with less than 40% of friends and family sharing AR (n=67) Q: How many", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-008", "title": "Brands and people connect better with AR experiences34% ", "content": "Brands and people connect better with AR experiences34% \n1: 2021 Global Deloitte Digital Study commissioned by Snap Inc.  Base = People who use AR all the time (n=135) Q: How familiar are you with AR? | A: I use AR all the time | Q: Sometimes brands will create AR experiences through an app camera (e.g., a big movie company makes an AR experience where the heroes of a movie are fighting next to you, or a restaurant provides you a lens/filter/effect that turns you into a hamburger). What impact, if any, does interacting with that AR experience have on your perception of that brand? | A: I am mo", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
