    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Arabia_Report_-029", "title": "37About DeloitteDeloitte refers to one or more of Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu Limit", "content": "37About DeloitteDeloitte refers to one or more of Deloitte Touche Tohmatsu Limited, a UK private company limited by guarantee (“DTTL”), its network of member firms, and their related entities. DTTL and each of its member firms are legally separate and independent entities. DTTL (also referred to as “Deloitte Global”) does not provide services to clients. In the United States, Deloitte refers to one or more of the US member firms of DTTL, their related entities that operate using the “Deloitte” name in the United States and their respective affiliates. Certain services may not be available to a", "source": "market/Saudi_Arabia_Report_Snap_Consumer_AR.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-001", "title": "Our culture, our identity", "content": "Our culture, our identity\nfashion.moc.gov.sa\nSaudi Arabia’s\nFashion Frontier:\neCommerce’s evolution \nand AI adoption\n\n2\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024Our culture, our identity\nfashion.moc.gov.saSaudi Arabia’s\nFashion Frontier:\neCommerce’s evolution \nand AI adoption", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-002", "title": "3Contents", "content": "3Contents\nExecutive summary   4\nInternational eCommerce trends   6 \nFashion’s Digital Revolution: Unveiling the Origins and Evolution of eCommerce in the Industry  6\nDressing Up the Bottom Line: Navigating Fashion eCommerce Revenue and T rends   6 \nFashion eCommerce Front-Runners: Investigating the Strategies and Drivers Behind Their Global T riumph 8 \nThreads in Demand: Delving into the Fashion Category with the Peak Online Shopping Volume  9 \nAge & Style: Mapping the Landscape of Fashion eCommerce Adoption Across Generations and Categories 11 \nStyle in Flux: A Comparative Analysis of Fashion", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-003", "title": "4", "content": "4\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024Executive \nSummary\nDeveloping Saudi Arabia’s digital economy is \na Vision 2030 strategic imperative1. Through \nthe National T ransformation Program, Saudi \nArabia seeks to derive 19.2% of GDP from the \ndigital economy by 20252. This White Paper \nanalyses the most recent global trends in the \nfashion eCommerce ecosystem, quantifies \nSaudi Arabia’s fashion eCommerce opportunity \nand identifies key support levers to accelerate \ngrowth. The analysis is derived from public fashion \nretailers’ integrated annual reports, investor \nannouncements, academic literature, ma", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-004", "title": "5", "content": "5\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption proportion of Saudi Arabia’s population being under the age of forty, \nan age group which represent the largest number of online shoppers \nglobally7. In Saudi Arabia, 65% of the population is under the age \nof forty. In China, the United States and Europe, the comparative \nportions are much lower at 51%, 54%, and 46% respectively8. \nGlobally, Generation Z (born between 1997-2012) and Millennials \n(born between 1981-1996) are increasingly inspired by online \ninfluencers, purchasing products via social media platforms. \nT e", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-005", "title": "6", "content": "6\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024\nInternational eCommerce \ntrends\neCommerce, an abbreviation of the term ‘electronic \ncommerce’ , is defined as the exchange of goods \nand services, and the transmission of funds and \ndata over the internet15. eCommerce traditionally \nrefers to business-to-consumer (B2C) or business-\nto-business (B2B) commerce. Following the \nintroduction of the World Wide Web in 1991 and \nwide adoption of the Internet globally from 1993, \neCommerce tracks its origin to a 1994 online \ntransaction of a US$12.48 CD sold in the United \nStates of America16. Since this first purchase,", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-006", "title": "7", "content": "7\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Online retail sales from 2022 to 2027 are projected to grow most significantly in the Middle East and Africa \n(15.75%), followed by Latin America (12.2%) and Asia Pacific (9.3%) (Figure 2). Growth is primarily driven \nby increased internet access in these regions and improved logistics infrastructure, which has improved \nconsumer ability to purchase products online26.FIGURE 1:\nFashion eCommerce retail value, US$ millions.\nSOURCE: EUROMONITOR (2023)\nFIGURE 2:\nProjected compound annual growth rate of online retail sales, 20", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-007", "title": "8", "content": "8\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024Fashion eCommerce front-runners: investigating the \nstrategies and drivers behind their global triumph\nLeaders in eCommerce comprise \na mix of mass fashion retailers \nand luxury groups such as \nInditex, H&M, L VMH and Kering, \nas well as digital-first firms \nsuch as Amazon, ASOS and \nBoohoo. More recently, the \nindustry has been disrupted by \nthe emergence of Chinese fast \nfashion brand Shein, valued at \nUS$100 billion27, which recorded \nUS$700 million in profits in 2022. \nShein is targeting US$60 billion \nin total sales by 202528. Despite \nrapid growth, Shein h", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-008", "title": "9", "content": "9\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption customer navigation of the \nwebsite44 . As stated by Inditex in \ntheir latest integrated directors’ \nreport, “We will continue to offer \nthe best shopping experience to \nour customers, both in our stores \nand on our online platforms45 .”\nEven digital-first retailers such as \nShein are recognizing the need \nfor an omnichannel strategy, by \ninvesting in pop-up stores, and \npartnering with US brand Forever \n21 to expand their physical \nfootprint in malls46 . The firm \nis also planning to open thirty \nGlobally, apparel accoun", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-009", "title": "10", "content": "10\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024For women’s eCommerce apparel \ncategories, trousers are the most \npopular product category sold \nonline at US$39 billion globally54. \nSecond are jerseys, sweatshirts \n& pullovers at US$34, followed \nby dresses & skirts at US$28 \nbillion55. ‘Tights and leggings are projected to see the largest \neCommerce sales growth, at \n55% from 2023 to 202756. Again, \nthis appears to be driven by the \nrise in leisurewear , sports attire \nand shapewear . However , it is \nworth noting that this is off a low \nbase of US$3 billion57.\nFor both men and women, suits appear to be the", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-010", "title": "11", "content": "11\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Age and style: mapping the landscape of fashion \neCommerce adoption across generations and categories\nClick to cart: unraveling the motivations behind online \npurchase decisionsStyle in flux: a comparative analysis of fashion \neCommerce landscapes before and after Covid-19Both Millennials (born between 1981-1996) and Generation Z (born between 1997-2012) are likely to \npurchase fashion from a social media platform, with 82% of Millennial shoppers and 52% of Generation Z \nshoppers purchasing fashion online in 202260. \nWhi", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-011", "title": "12", "content": "12\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024The game-changing influence of AI in fashion \neCommerce\nArtificial intelligencea investment has increased across the fashion ecosystem67. Its application in \neCommerce has varied in the past 5 years, highlighting three key avenues of use: customer experience \nenhancement, marketing and supply chain optimization.\n•  Customer experience enhancement: AI models can be used to elevate the consumer’s shopping \nexperience by creating a personalized online consumer journey. Offers are then based on individual \nconsumer profiles while AI-enabled virtual assistants provi", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-012", "title": "13", "content": "13\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Developing Saudi \nArabia’s digital \neconomy is a strategic \nimperative in the \nKingdom’s Vision 2030, \na blueprint for a more \ndiverse economy72. Through the National \nT ransformation Program, the \nKingdom aims to derive 19.2% of \nits GDP from the digital economy \nby 202573. The eCommerce \nsector is closely interconnected \nwith other sectors such as \nlogistics. As eCommerce \ngrows, these other sectors will also experience increased \ninvestments, facilitating more \njob opportunities74, driving a \npositive impact on Saudi ", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-013", "title": "14", "content": "14\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024FIGURE 6:\nApparel eCommerce subcategories, in US$m, 2023.\nSOURCE: ST A TIST A (2023)84Fashion unboxed: a comprehensive breakdown of \neCommerce market by product categories\nAligned with global trends, \nwomen’s apparel has a larger \neCommerce presence than \nmen’s apparel. Women’s apparel \nwas valued at US$647 million77  \nin 2023, compared to men’s \napparel, which was valued at \nUS$357 million78  (Figure 6). This \nis a large divide when compared \nto global trends, which may \nindicate that online men’s apparel \nchannels may be under-explored \nlocally.\nDelving furth", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-014", "title": "15", "content": "15\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption A comparative analysis of online fashion sales and brick-\nand-mortar growth\nA full 74% of fashion sales are still \nexpected to take place in physical \nstores in Saudi Arabia by 2027 \n(Figure 7). \nThis suggests shoppers in Saudi \nArabia will continue to value in-\nstore purchases more than the \naverage global shopper . Saudi \nArabia’s state of the art shopping \nmalls are expected to continue \nBrands weighing their investment \nbetween online and physical \nsales channels should consider a \nstrategy that doesn’t exclusively \n", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-015", "title": "16", "content": "16\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024Is mCommerce and social shopping the future of \neCommerce in Saudi Arabia?\nThe key players in eCommerce in the KingdomThe percentage of Saudis with \nsmart phones is high (98% in \n2022)93 , well beyond the Middle \nEast and North Africa average \n(MENA, 76%)94 and above the \nglobal average (68%) 95. This \nsuggests a considerable market \nfor commerce via mobile phone \napplications (mCommerce). \nIn Saudi Arabia however , this \nremains a relatively untapped \nopportunity, with only 39% of \nall eCommerce sales generated \nthrough mobile phones in 202396 . \nThis percenta", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-016", "title": "17", "content": "17\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Chalhoub Group considers \nits strategic partnership with \nthe General Authority for Civil \nAviation (GACA) and a planned \nfulfilment center in Riyadh’s \nSpecial Integrated Logistics \nZone (SILZ) as pivotal steps \nin enhancing the Group’s \nomnichannel distribution \ncapabilities. Amit Keswani, \nChief Omnichannel Officer \nexplains: “Omnichannel is \nmore than a strategy; it’s \nan ethos. Our commitment \nto an integrated customer \njourney—from browsing and \npurchase to delivery and \naftercare—is fundamental in \ndriving the Gro", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-017", "title": "18", "content": "18\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024storage, the group is building a \ntechnology hub in Riyadh and has \nidentified the possibility of using \nvacant space in existing shopping \nmalls as micro-fulfilment centres \nwhere customers can collect \ntheir orders106 . \nNamshi has an established \npresence in Saudi Arabia, with \nthe country contributing over \n70% of its total sales107 . Its \nsuccess can be partly attributed \nto the continued demand for \nCash-on-Delivery services in the \nKingdom108 . Namshi’s growth has \nbeen driven by promoting online \nshopping via social media, using \ncustomer-created conten", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-018", "title": "19", "content": "19\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Navigating the eCommerce \nHorizon for the T op 100 Saudi \nBrands - Present and Future \nPerspectives\nVoices in data: analyzing survey responses for in-depth \ninsights\nA 2023 survey of 15 Saudi 100 brandsb companies assessed the current and forecasted rate of fashion \neCommerce uptake to understand opportunities and challenges the brands currently face with \neCommerce adoption. The survey found that 93% of the brands aim to increase their online sales in the \nnext three years. On average, respondents anticipated that 66% o", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-019", "title": "20", "content": "20\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024Strategies for emerging local fashion brands to flourish in \neCommerce\nT ype of product is a principal \nfactor when building an \neCommerce strategy. As \ndiscussed in the global chapter , \ncustomers tend to prefer physical \nshopping for suits and luxury \nproducts. However , regardless of \nthe type of product, consumers \nare increasingly engaging online \nto discover and research brands.\n58% of surveyed retail \nprofessionals indicated an intent \nto invest in ensuring consumers \ncan directly purchase from \nsocial media channels while \nmore than half indicated plann", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-020", "title": "21", "content": "21\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Online retailers operating in the \nKingdom currently offer their \ncustomers payment through a \nvariety of methods that include, \nbut are not limited to, ApplePay, \nPay Pal and various credit and \ndebit card providers.d There are \nalso ‘buy now pay later’ options \nby payment gateways service \nproviders such as T amara and \nT abby which, according to the \nMinistry of Commerce, are \nbecoming increasingly popular in \nthe Kingdom. Cash on Delivery \n(COD) is also still offered by \nmany retailers in the Kingdom. \nOfficially, th", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-021", "title": "22", "content": "22\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024and pick up in store (BOPIS), \nwhile in contrast, lockers \noffer a convenient alternative \nby providing a secure and \ncentralized location for parcels \nto be delivered to or picked up \nfrom. When making a purchase, \ncustomers have the option of \nselecting a convenient locker \nnear them where they can pick \nup their delivery or place an item \nthat they would like to return. In some instances, online retailers \nare incentivizing the use of this \nsystem over home delivery. They \naccomplish this through free \ndelivery to a locker . This is the \ncase for popular onl", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-022", "title": "23", "content": "23\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Click, shop, delight: strategies for elevating consumer \nexperiences in online shopping\nEnsuring consumers have an excellent user experience when shopping online is essential to developing \ncustomer loyalty and attracting new customers. A key measure of this success is improving conversion rates, \nwhich is the ability to take a customer through the full suite of an eCommerce experience: from visiting the \nsite, finding and selecting a product, to finally purchasing it. Below are the top five ways to improve online \nshopp", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-023", "title": "24", "content": "24\nOur Culture, Our Identity 2024T op four lessons from international fashion eCommerce \nmarkets\n1. Delivering a seamless omnichannel experience.\nStudies show the importance of having both an online and an in-store market presence. Globally, the \nmajority of shoppers (73%) use multiple channels (online and offline) during their customer journey146. \nImportantly, these shoppers tend to spend more than consumers who use only a single channel147. \nEnsuring that customers have a seamless experience both in-store and online is important to maximizing \ncustomer satisfaction levels and to driving onl", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
    {"id": "MARKET-Saudi_Fashion_Fronti-024", "title": "25", "content": "25\nSaudi Arabia’s Fashion Frontier: eCommerce’s Evolution and AI Adoption Fashion eCommerce \nis likely to develop in \nunanticipated ways \nover the next few years.\nOngoing experimentation \ndriven by rapidly evolving \ncustomer requirements and \nintensifying competition in \nthe online and in-store space \nwill see the emergence of new \ndevelopments that improve \ncustomer digital interfaces with \neCommerce platforms, create \nnew in-store experiences, and \nensure products and services \nare delivered to customers \nmore accurately and within ever \nshorter lead times. The rapid \ngrowth and changing pro", "source": "market/Saudi_Fashion_Frontier_2024.pdf", "dimension": "market", "tags": []},
